\id 1KI - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.23
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Kings
\toc1 The first summary of the Kings of Israel and Judah
\toc2 1 Kings
\mt2 The first summary of the
\mt1 Kings of Israel and Judah
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 David in his old age
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now King David had become very old, and he couldn’t keep warm even when covered with blankets,
\v 2 so his servants suggested, “Allow us search for a young woman who can wait on you and take care of your needs. She can sleep beside you and keep our master, the king, warm.”
\v 3 So they searched throughout Israel and found a beautiful young woman called Avishag (a Shunamit) and brought her to the king.
\v 4 She was incredibly beautiful and she took care of the king, but he didn’t have sexual relations with her.
\s1 Adonijah tries to become king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 5 Then \add David and\add* Hagit’s son Adonijah promoted himself saying, “I, myself, will reign.” He acquired a chariot and horsemen and fifty men who ran along in front.
\v 6 Hagit had given birth to him after Absalom. He was a good-looker, \add but\add* his father had never disciplined him \add or ever\add* asked, “Why did you do that?”
\v 7 He had spoken with Tseruyah’s son Joab and the priest Abiathar and they had \add both\add* agreed to support him.
\v 8 But the priest Zadok and Yehoyada’s son Benayah, the prophet Nathan, Shimei and Re’i, and David’s top warriors were \add all\add* against Adonijah.
\p
\v 9 Then Adonijah sacrificed sheep and cattle and fattened calves near the Zohelet stone (which is beside Eyn-Rogel), and he invited all his brothers (the king’s sons), and all the men of Judah \add including\add* the king’s servants.
\v 10 However, he didn’t invite the prophet Nathan, Benayah, the top warriors, or his brother Solomon.
\s1 Solomon is made king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 11 Then Nathan asked Solomon’s mother Bathsheba, “Haven’t you heard that Hagit’s son Adonijah has made himself king, and our master David doesn’t \add even\add* realise?
\v 12 So please let me advise you now so that you can save your own life and that of your son Solomon.
\v 13 Go and ask King David, ‘My master the king, didn’t you promise your female servant, saying, “Surely your son Solomon will reign after me, and he will sit on my throne?” So why has Adonijah become king?’
\v 14 Then while you’re still there talking to the king, I’ll come in behind you and confirm what you said.”
\p
\v 15 So Bathsheba went to the king’s bedroom where Avishag (the Shunamit) was attending him in his old age.
\v 16 Bathsheba bowed low to show respect to the king, and he asked her what she wanted.
\p
\v 17 “My master,” she replied, “you yourself promised to your female servant in front of your god Yahweh that your son Solomon would be the one to reign after you and sit on your throne.
\v 18 But now, listen, Adonijah has become king \add instead\add*, and you haven’t even been told about it.
\v 19 He’s sacrificing many oxen and calves and sheep, and he invited the priest Abiathar, and Joab the commander of the army, and all your sons except for your servant Solomon.
\v 20 So \add now\add* everyone’s waiting to see what you’ll do, my master the king. They want you to make it clearly known who’ll be the king on the throne after you.
\v 21 Otherwise, as soon as my master the king \add passes on\add* to join his ancestors, my son Solomon and I will be considered to be ‘in the way’.”
\p
\v 22 Just then, while she was still talking with the king, the prophet Nathan appeared
\v 23 and his arrival was announced to the king. He went into the room and knelt in front of the king and bowed his face to the floor.
\v 24 Then he asked, “My master the king, did you announce that Adonijah will reign after you and sit on your throne?
\v 25 \add I ask\add* because today he sacrificed plenty of oxen and calves and sheep. He’d invited all your sons and army commanders, and Abiathar the priest. They’re \add all\add* eating and drinking with him and saying, ‘Long live King Adonijah!’
\v 26 But as for me, I’m \em your\em* servant, and nor did he invite Zadok the priest or Yehoyada’s son Benayah, or Solomon your servant.
\v 27 Did my master the king organise that without telling your servants who will sit on the throne of my master the king after him?”
\p
\v 28 Then King David said, “Call Bathsheba \add back in\add*.” So she came \add back\add* into the king’s \add room\add* and stood in front of him.
\v 29 Then the king promised, “As Yahweh lives and has rescued me from every danger,
\v 30 I promise you \add in front of\add* Israel’s god Yahweh that your son Solomon will reign after me and will sit on my throne in my place. I’ll put it in action today.”
\p
\v 31 Bathsheba knelt down and bowed her face to the floor and said, “May my master, the king David live forever.”
\p
\v 32 Then King David called out, “Summon Zadok the priest for me, and Nathan the prophet, and Yehoyada’s son Benayah.” So they came into the king’s room.
\v 33 “Take some of my servants,” the king instructed them. “and put my son Solomon on my mule and lead him to the Gihon \add spring\add*.
\v 34 Then \add you two,\add* Zadok and Nathan, must anoint him there as king over Israel. Then blow the horn and shout, ‘Let King Solomon live!’
\v 35 Then follow him back here where he must come and sit on my throne. Then he’ll reign in my place as ruler of Israel and Judah.”
\p
\v 36 “Certainly,” Benayah replied. “Just as Yahweh, the god of my master the king has instructed.
\v 37 Just as Yahweh has been with my master the king, so may he be with Solomon. And may his rule be \add even\add* greater than that of my master, the king David.”
\p
\v 38 So Zadok the priest and Nathan the prophet and Yehoyada’s son Benayah and the kings bodyguards went and put Solomon on the king’s mule, and took him to Gihon.
\v 39 Zadok the priest took the horn of oil from the \add sacred\add* tent and anointed Solomon \add with it\add*, and they blew the horn and all the people shouted, “Let King Solomon live!”
\v 40 Then all the people followed him \add back\add* uphill \add to the city\add*, and the people were playing flutes and were so exuberant in their celebrations that the ground shook with the noise.
\p
\v 41 When Adonijah and all his invited guests had finished eating, they heard the noise, and Joab heard the sound of the horn and asked, “What’s all that commotion in the city?”
\v 42 He was still speaking when Jonathan the son of Abiathar the priest \add suddenly turned up\add*, and Adonijah called him, “Come \add over here\add* because you’re a good fellow and you’ll bring good news.”
\p
\v 43 “\add Actually, no,\add*” Jonathan answered. “Our master the king David has \add just\add* made Solomon king.
\v 44 The king sent Zadok the priest and Nathan the prophet and Yehoyada’s son Benayah and the king’s bodyguards, and they placed Solomon on the king’s mule.
\v 45 Then Zadok and Nathan anointed him as king at Gihon. \add Now\add* they’ve gone back from there cheering \add all the way\add*, and the \add whole\add* city is \add happily\add* celebrating. That’s the noise that you’re all hearing.
\v 46 What’s more, Solomon’s sitting on the royal throne \add right now\add*.
\v 47 As well as that, the king’s servants went in to bless our master the king David, saying, ‘May God make Solomon’s reputation \add even\add* better than yours, and may he make his reign \add even\add* greater than yours.’ Then the king in his bed, bowed down
\v 48 and said, ‘Blessed be Israel’s god Yahweh who has provided \add a son\add* to sit on my throne today, and I’ve been able to see it \add before my death\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 49 Then all Adonijah’s invited guests started trembling, and they all got up and quietly slipped back to their homes.
\v 50 Adonijah \add himself\add* was now afraid of what Solomon would do, so he went \add to the sacred tent\add* and held on to the projecting corners of the altar.
\v 51 Solomon was told, “Listen, Adonijah is afraid of the new king because he’s grabbed the projecting corners of the altar and said, ‘Let Solomon the king promise me as soon as possible, that he won’t execute his servant with the sword.’ ”
\p
\v 52 “If he shows himself as a loyal subject,” Solomon responded, “then he’ll be able to keep his head. But if he’s found to be a traitor, then he’ll die.”
\v 53 So King Solomon sent \add for Adonijah\add*, and they brought him down from beside the altar. He came and bowed down to Solomon the king and Solomon told him, “Go home.”
\c 2
\s1 David’s final words to Solomon
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 When David knew that his death was close, he instructed his son Solomon,
\v 2 “I’ll soon be coming to my end, but \add you must be\add* strong and sensible.
\v 3 You must maintain your god Yahweh’s charge to walk in his ways, to keep his statutes, his commands, his judgements, and his testimonies, according to what is written in Moses’ instructions, so that you’ll be wise in everything you do and everywhere that you turn,
\v 4 so that Yahweh’s promise to me that he’d keep one of my descendants on Israel’s throne, will stand because he gave the condition, ‘If your descendants will obey me and worship me in truth with all their hearts and with all their souls.’
\p
\v 5 “Also you’re aware of what Tseruyah’s son Joab did to me—what he did to two of the commanders of Israel’s armies—to Ner’s son Abner and to Yeter’s son Amasa. He murdered them, shedding blood in peace\add -time\add* as if it was in a war, and the blood guilt is on him from head to toe.
\v 6 So use your \add own\add* wisdom, but don’t let Joab get old and die peacefully.
\p
\v 7 However, return loyal kindness to the sons of Barzilay the Giladi, and invite them to join you at your table because they helped me when I was fleeing from your brother Absalom.
\p
\v 8 Then there’s Gera’s son Shimei, the Benyamite from Bahurim. He cursed me horribly when I was going to Mahanayim. Then he went down to meet me at the Jordan, and I promised him by Yahweh that I wouldn’t execute him,
\v 9 so don’t leave him unpunished now. You’re a wise man and you’ll know how to handle him. Although he’s old, make sure he has a bloody death.
\s1 David’s death
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then David breathed his last and was buried in David’s city.
\v 11 He had reigned over Israel for forty years—seven years from Hebron and thirty-three years from Jerusalem.
\v 12 Solomon replaced his father David on the throne, and his authority was established with firmness.
\s1 Adonijah’s provocation and death
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 13 Then Hagit’s son Adonijah came to Solomon’s mother Bathsheba and she asked him, “Is your coming peaceful?”
\p “It is,” he replied.
\v 14 “Can I ask you something?”
\p “Go ahead,” she answered.
\p
\v 15 “You yourself know that the kingship was for me \add as the oldest son\add*,” he said, “and all Israel wanted me to reign. But then Yahweh decided that it was for my brother, and then everything got turned around.
\v 16 So now, I have only one thing to request from you—please don’t refuse me.”
\p “Go on,” she said.
\p
\v 17 He said, “Please speak to Solomon the king because he wouldn’t refuse you. Ask him to give me Avishag the Shunamit as my wife.”
\p
\v 18 “Okay,” Bathsheba agreed, “I’ll personally speak to the king for you.”
\p
\v 19 So Bathsheba went to King Solomon to speak to him on Adonijah’s behalf. The king rose to meet her and bowed down to her. Then he sat on his throne and he had a throne placed on his right for his mother to sit on.
\v 20 Then she said, “I have one small request for you. \add Please\add* don’t say you won’t do it”
\p “\add What’s\add* your request, my mother,” the king responded, “because I couldn’t refuse you.”
\p
\v 21 “Let Avishag the Shunamit,” she said, “be given to your brother Adonijah as a wife.”
\p
\v 22 King Solomon answered his mother, “You’re requesting Avishag the Shunamit for Adonijah! You might as well request the throne for him, because he’s my brother and he’s older than I am! And then \add he’ll want\add* Abiathar the priest and Tseruyah’s son Joab!”
\v 23 Then Solomon promised by Yahweh, “May God do to me whatever he wants, unless Adonijah pays with his life for saying that.
\v 24 And now as Yahweh lives, the one who established me and caused me to sit on the throne of my father David, and who promised David’s descendants to continue reigning, Adonijah will certainly be put to death today.”
\p
\v 25 So King Solomon instructed Yehoyada’s son Benayah and he \add went and\add* struck Adonijah down and he died.
\s1 Abiathar’s banishment and Joab’s death
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 26 Then concerning Abiathar the priest, the king told him, “Go to your fields at Anatot because you’re marked for death, but I won’t do it today because you carried my master Yahweh’s \add sacred\add* box ahead of my father David, and because you suffered through all that my father suffered.”
\v 27 \add That was how\add* Solomon \add banished\add* Abiathar from being a priest to Yahweh. \add It was done\add* to fulfill what Yahweh had said at Shiloh said about \add removing\add* Eli’s descendants.
\p
\v 28 When the news of that reached Joab, he fled to Yahweh’s tent and held onto the projecting corners of the altar, because he had supported Adonijah (even though he hadn’t supported Absalom.
\v 29 \add Someone\add* told King Solomon that Joab had fled to Yahweh’s tent and was \add there\add* beside the altar, so Solomon instructed Yehoyada’s son Benayah, “Go and strike him down.”
\v 30 So Benayah entered Yahweh’s tent and he told Joab, “The king said for you to come out.”
\p “No, I’ll die here,” he replied.
\p So Benayah went back to the king and reported both sides of the conversation
\v 31 and the king told him, “Just do what he said. Kill him and bury him, and \add in that way\add* you’ll remove the blood guilt from me and my descendants for the blood that Joab shed without cause.
\v 32 Yahweh will return his blood onto his \add own\add* head, because he attacked two men more righteous and better than himself: Ner’s son Abner, the commander of Israel’s army, and Yeter’s son Amasa, the commander of Judah’s army. He killed them with the sword and my father David didn’t have anything to do with it.
\v 33 Their shed blood will return onto Joab’s head and on the heads of his descendants forever. But for David and his family and his descendants and his throne, Yahweh will give peace forever.”
\p
\v 34 So Yehoyada’s son Benayah went and struck Joab \add at the altar\add* and killed him. Then he was buried on his \add property\add* in the wilderness.
\v 35 The king appointed Benayah as army commander to replace Joab, and appointed Zadok as priest in place of Abiathar.
\s1 Shimei’s execution
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 36 Then the king summoned Shimei and told him, “Build yourself a house \add here\add* in Jerusalem and live there. But don’t go anywhere else
\v 37 because the day that you leave and cross the Kidron valley, you can be certain that you’ll die. Your blood will be on your \add own\add* head.”
\p
\v 38 “My master the king,” Shimei responded, “your decision is good and your servant will do just what you said.”
\p
\v 39 However, three years \add later\add*, two of Shimei’s slaves fled to Ma’akah’s son, King Akish of Gath, and Shimei was told where they’d gone.
\v 40 So Shimei saddled his donkey and rode it to Akish at Gath to try to find his slaves and he was able to recover them
\v 41 But Solomon was told that Shimei \add left\add* Jerusalem and gone to Gath and then returned.
\v 42 So the king summoned Shimei and asked him, “Didn’t I get you to promise by Yahweh that you heard me say that whenever you leave here, you’ll certainly die? And didn’t you tell me that it was a good decision?
\v 43 So why didn’t you keep the promise by Yahweh and \add obey\add* what I instructed you?
\v 44 You yourself know all the evil things that you did to my father David, so now Yahweh will return your evil on your head.
\v 45 But \add despite what you yelled out,\add* \add Yahweh\add* will bless me as king, and David’s throne will be established forever in front of Yahweh.”
\p
\v 46 Then the king commanded Yehoyada’s son Benayah, and he went out and put Shimei to death.
\p \add In those ways,\add* Solomon established his control of the kingdom.
\c 3
\s1 Solomon requests wisdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Solomon made himself the son-in-law of Egypt’s king Pharaoh by taking Pharaoh’s daughter as a wife and bringing her into David’s city (\add where she stayed\add* until the completion of the construction of his palace and of Yahweh’s temple, and the wall around Jerusalem.
\v 2 However, the people were sacrificing on various hills because at that time, a temple hadn’t \add yet\add* been built for Yahweh.
\v 3 Solomon \add demonstrated his\add* love for Yahweh by obeying the instructions of his father David. However, he would \add offer\add* sacrifices and burn incense on various hills.
\p
\v 4 \add One time,\add* the king went to Gibeon to sacrifice there, because it was the most popular high place. Solomon offered up a thousand burnt up sacrifices on that altar,
\v 5 and that night in Gibeon, Yahweh appeared to Solomon in a dream and asked, “What would you like me to give you?”
\p
\v 6 Solomon replied, “You yourself showed incredible loyalty to your servant David, my father, because he walked in faithfulness and obedience in front of you, and did what he believed you wanted. And you’ve maintained this loyalty towards him and you allowed him to have a son sitting on his throne as I am today.
\v 7 So now, Yahweh my god, you yourself have caused your servant to reign in place of my father David, but I’m still young—I don’t really know much about anything.
\v 8 Yet your servant is among your chosen people—a numerous people who can’t be counted because there’s so many \add of us\add*.
\v 9 So please give your servant the ability to listen to your people and to judge them well—discerning between good and evil, because who’s able to judge your people—that’s a heavy task.”
\p
\v 10 Yahweh was very pleased that Solomon had requested that
\v 11 and told him, “Because you requested that and didn’t request a long life or riches for yourself, or requested the lives of your enemies, but you requested discernment for yourself when listening and dispensing justice,
\v 12 then I’ve done what you requested. Listen, I’ve given you a wise and discerning mind unlike anyone who’s lived before you or who will live in the future after you.
\v 13 So I’ve also given you what you didn’t request: both riches and honour—there’ll be no other king like you for as long as you live,
\v 14 and if you’ll obey my statutes and commands like your father David did, then I’ll \add also\add* lengthen your life.”
\p
\v 15 Then Solomon woke up, and wow, it had been a dream, and he went \add into\add* Jerusalem and stood in front of the sacred chest. Then he offered up burnt offerings and made peace offerings, and he held a feast for all his servants.
\s1 Solomon judges a difficult case
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 16 One day two prostitutes came to the king and stood in front of him,
\v 17 and one of them said, “Please, my master, us two women live in the same house, and while she was there in the house, I gave birth \add to a baby\add*.
\v 18 Three days later, this woman also gave birth \add to her baby\add*. Only the two of us were there in the house—no one else was around.
\v 19 \add But\add* then in the night, her son died because she rolled over onto him.
\v 20 So she got up in the middle of the night and took my son from beside me, as your female servant was asleep. She left her dead son with me, and took my son to her bed.
\v 21 When I woke up in the morning \add ready\add* to breastfeed my son, to my horror he was dead. \add However\add* when I examined him closely in the light, more shock, it wasn’t \add actually\add* my son!”
\p
\v 22 “No, my son is the living one,” the other woman said, “and your son is the dead one.”
\p “No, \em your\em* son is the dead one,” the first woman countered, “and \em my\em* son is the living one!”
\p Then they \add continued to\add* argue in front of the king.
\p
\v 23 The king spoke, “So both of you are claiming that your baby is the one that’s alive and that the dead one belongs to the other.”
\v 24 Then he ordered, “Get me a sword.” So they brought a sword to the king.
\v 25 Then the king commanded, “Cut the living child into two pieces and give them half each.”
\p
\v 26 But the mother of the surviving baby felt compassion for her son and yelled, “Please, my master, give the baby to her—you certainly mustn’t kill him!”
\p Meanwhile the other woman was saying, “Well then, neither of us will have him. Cut him in half!”
\p
\v 27 The king spoke up, “Don’t harm the baby. Give the living child to the \add first\add* woman—she’s his mother.”
\p
\v 28 All Israel \add eventually\add* heard about that case that the king had judged, and they felt an awesome respect for him because they saw that he had God’s wisdom to do justice within him.
\c 4
\s1 Solomon’s officials
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 So Solomon was king over all Israel,
\v 2 and these were his officials:
\q1 Zadok’s son Azaryah was the priest.
\q1
\v 3 Shisha’s sons Elihoref and Ahiyah were secretaries.
\q1 Ahilud’s son Jehoshaphat was the recorder.
\q1
\v 4 Yehoyada’s son Benayah was the army commander.
\q1 Zadok and Abiathar were priests.
\q1
\v 5 Nathan’s son Azaryah was supervisor of the governors,
\q1 and his son Zavud was a priest and adviser to the king.
\q1
\v 6 Ahishar was in charge of the palace,
\q1 and Avda’s son Adoniram supervised the forced labourers.
\p
\v 7 Solomon appointed twelve governors over all Israel, and they \add sent in\add* food for the palace—each governor was responsible for one month of the year.
\v 8 Their names were:
\q1 Ben-Hur in the Ephraim hill country,
\q1
\v 9 Ben-Deker in Makats, Sha’alvim, Beyt-Shemesh, and Elon-Beyt-Hanan,
\q1
\v 10 Ben-Hesed in Arubot, Sokoh, and all of Hefer,
\q1
\v 11 Ben-Avinadav (who was married to Solomon’s daughter Tafat) in Nafat-Dor,
\q1
\v 12 Ahilud’s son Ba’ana in Ta’anak and Megiddo and all Beyt-Shean next to Tseretan below Jezreel, from Beyt-Shean to Abel-Meholah, as far as the other side of Yakemeam,
\q1
\v 13 Ben-Gever in Ramot-Gilead, including the villages of Manasseh’s son Yair that were in Gilead, as well as the Argov area in the Bashan region, and including sixty large cities with walls and bronze \add gate\add* bars.
\q1
\v 14 Ido’s son Ahinadav in Mahanayim.
\q1
\v 15 Ahima’ats (who had married Solomon’s daughter Basemat) in Naphtali,
\q1
\v 16 Hushay’s son Ba’ana in Asher and Be’alot,
\q1
\v 17 Paruah’s son Jehoshaphat in Issachar,
\q1
\v 18 Elah’s son Shimei in Benjamin,
\q1
\v 19 Uri’s son Gever in the Gilead region, \add including\add* the lands of the Amorite King Sihon and King Og of the Bashan.
\p There was also one district governor over Judah.
\s1 Solomon’s prosperity
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 20 \add Now\add* Judah and Israel had become as numerous as the sand on the seashore. They \add had plenty\add* to eat and drink and they were contented.
\v 21 Solomon controlled all the kingdoms from the \add Euphrates\add* river to the land of the Philistines and as far as the Egyptian border, \add so they\add* brought tribute to him and followed his orders during his lifetime.
\p
\v 22 The daily requirements for the palace included five tonnes of fine flour and eleven tonnes of coarse flour,
\v 23 ten fattened cattle, twenty pasture cattle, one hundred sheep, \add wild game including\add* deer, gazelle, and roebuck, and fattened fowls.
\p
\v 24 He controlled all the kingdoms west of the Euphrates from Tifsah \add in the north-east\add* to Gaza \add in the south-west\add*, and there was peace with all his neighbours.
\v 25 So all Judah and Israel lived peacefully, from Dan \add in the north\add* to Beersheba \add in the south\add*—everyone focusing on their own property.
\p
\v 26 Solomon had forty thousand stalls for his chariot horses, and twelve thousand horsemen.
\v 27 The district governors provided supplies for the palace—one governor for each month. They ensured that nothing was lacking for the king and his visitors.
\v 28 They also provided the barley and straw for the chariot horses and the other horses, delivered to the designated locations.
\s1 Solomon’s wisdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 29 God gave Solomon wisdom and incredible understanding, and the breadth of his interests seemed without limit.
\v 30 He was wiser than anyone in the countries east of Israel or in Egypt.
\v 31 He was wiser than anyone else, including Eytan the Ezrahi and Mahol’s son Heyman and Kalkol and Darda, and his reputation spread to all the surrounding countries.
\v 32 He could tell three thousand proverbs and he \add composed\add* over a thousand songs.
\v 33 He could lecture on plant life from the cedars in Lebanon to the hyssop that grows on walls. He taught about animals, birds, reptiles, and fish.
\v 34 People from all around came to listen to Solomon’s wisdom—many having been sent by other kings who’d heard about him.
\c 5
\s1 The preparation of Solomon of making the temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 2:1-18)
\p
\v 1 Then King Hiram of Tyre sent his servants to Solomon when he heard that he’d been anointed as king to replace his father, because Hiram had always been a friend of David.
\v 2 Solomon replied to Hiram,
\v 3 “You yourself knew David my father, and you knew that he wasn’t able to build a temple for his god Yahweh his god because of the constant state of warfare until Yahweh would allow us to defeat all our enemies.
\v 4 But now, my god Yahweh has enabled us to have peace from all around. We have no enemy, and there’s no threat of danger.
\v 5 So listen, I’m planning to build a residence for my god Yahweh, just as Yahweh spoke to my father David, ‘Your son who I’ll put in your place on your throne, he’ll \add be the one to\add* build the house to honour me.’
\v 6 So now, \add please give the\add* command to fell cedars for me from the Lebanon \add forest\add*. Let my servants accompany your servants and I’ll pay your servants’ wages whatever amount you set, because you yourself know that there are no other workers who know how to fell trees like the Sidonians.”
\p
\v 7 When Hiram got Solomon’s message, he was very happy and said, “Blessed be Yahweh today, who has given David a wise son \add to rule\add* over that great nation.”
\v 8 He sent \add this message back\add* to Solomon, “I’ve received the message that you sent me and I myself will supervise everything you want including cedar and cypress logs.
\v 9 My servants will transport them down from the forest to the ocean, and I’ll get them tied into rafts and float them to the place you specify and disassemble the rafts. Then it’ll be up to you to get them where you need them. All I ask for, is food for my family and workers.”
\p
\v 10 So Hiram supplied all the cedar and cypress logs that Solomon ordered
\v 11 and each year Solomon supplied Hiram with three thousand tonnes of wheat and four hundred thousand litres of pure \add olive\add* oil.
\v 12 Yahweh gave Solomon wisdom just like he’d said he would, and there was peace between Hiram and Solomon and the two of them made a treaty with each other.
\p
\v 13 King Solomon forced thirty thousand men from all over Israel to become his workers,
\v 14 and each month he sent a group of ten thousand of them to \add work in\add* Lebanon. Each group would spend a month in Lebanon and then two months at home \add again\add*. Adoniram was put in charge of all that.
\v 15 Solomon \add also\add* forced eighty thousand men to cut stones in the hill country and seventy thousand men to haul loads.
\v 16 Then besides Solomon’s chief officers who were in charge of the work, there were \add another\add* three thousand three hundred supervisors of the workers.
\v 17 The king commanded them to quarry large, valuable blocks of dressed stone for the temple foundations.
\v 18 Solomon’s and Hiram’s workers and the Givlim shaped the stones and prepared the timber for building the temple.
\c 6
\s1 Solomon begins temple construction
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 So \add it was that\add* four hundred and eighty years after the Israelites left Egypt, in the second month of the fourth year of Solomon’s reign over Israel, he began the construction of Yahweh’s residence.
\v 2 It would be twenty-seven metres long, nine metres wide, and fourteen metres high.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft About the height of a modern five-storey building (and the same height as Noah’s barge).\f*
\v 3 The five-metre wide porch on the front was the same length as the temple width, so nine metres long.
\v 4 The window \add openings\add* narrowed \add towards the outer edge of the wall\add*.
\v 5 Against the outside of the back wall and the two sides, he built \add three-storey (but lower than the wall)\add* side rooms.
\v 6 The lowest level was just over two metres wide, the middle \add level\add* was half a metre wider, and the top level wider again \add (because the temple wall got thinner as it went up)\add*. The temple walls had ledges built into them for the beams to sit on, so they wouldn’t have to go through the walls.
\p
\v 7 While the temple was being built, all of the \add huge\add* stones were dressed at the quarry \add site\add*, so there was no noise from any hammer or axe or iron tool at the construction site.
\p
\v 8 The entrance to the bottom level of siderooms was on the south side of the temple, then from there, stairs went up to the middle level, and more stairs to the top level.
\v 9 So he built the temple \add walls\add*, then it was roofed with cedar beams and planks,
\v 10 and he built the siderooms—each level just over two metres high and attached to the temple with cedar \add beams\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh had this message sent to Solomon,
\v 12 “This residence that you’re building, if you comply with my regulations and follow my judgements and obey all my commands, then I’ll ensure that everything I promised your father David will come to pass.
\v 13 I will live among \add you\add* Israelites and I’ll never abandon my people Israel.”
\s1 The temple furnishings
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 3:8-14)
\p
\v 14 So Solomon finished the construction of the temple,
\v 15 and on the inside he made walls of cedar boards. The floor was made with cypress boards, and the walls were panelled from the floor to the ceiling.
\v 16 Inside the temple, nine metres at the rear \add were closed off\add* to make an inner room called ‘the very holy place’. That too had walls made of cedar boards.
\v 17 That left the main temple room as eighteen metres long,
\v 18 and it had cedar boards on the walls \add decorated with\add* carvings of buds and flowers in blossom. All the internal walls were covered with cedar so none of the large stones were visible.
\p
\v 19 The inner room at the back was to house the box containing Yahweh’s agreement,
\v 20 and the room (nine metres long and wide and high) was overlaid with pure gold and equipped with an \add incense\add* altar overlaid with cedar.
\v 21 Then Solomon overlaid the main room with pure gold, and he \add hung\add* gold chains crossing across the entrance to the inner room that was overlaid with gold.
\v 22 He overlaid \add the interior of\add* the entire temple with gold until it was all finished, including overlaying the altar belonging to the inner room with gold.
\p
\v 23 In the inner room, he made two winged creatures that were five metres high and made of olive wood.
\v 24 Each wing was over two metres long, giving each one a total wingspan of four and a half metres.
\v 25 Both creatures were the same size and shape,
\v 26 \add and\add* they were both five metres high.
\v 27 The \add two\add* winged creatures were placed across the inner room where their wingtips touched each other in the centre and their outer wings touched the walls.
\v 28 \add Both of\add* the wooden carvings were overlaid with gold.
\p
\v 29 He \add had\add* all the \add interior\add* walls of the temple engraved with winged creatures and palm trees and flowers in bloom,
\v 30 and he overlaid the floor of both rooms with gold.
\p
\v 31 He made doors and doorposts for the inner room with olive wood,
\v 32 and both doors were decorated with carvings of winged creatures and palm trees and flowers in bloom, then they were overlaid with gold that was hammered over the carvings.
\v 33 He made the doorposts for the main temple entrance with olive wood,
\v 34 and the two folding doors themselves each had two folding sections made of cypress.
\v 35 \add These doors were also decorated with\add* carvings of winged creatures and palm trees and flowers in bloom, and then overlaid with gold smoothed down over the carvings.
\p
\v 36 He built the inner courtyard with three rows of dressed stones and a row of cedar beams.
\p
\v 37 The foundation for Yahweh’s residence was laid in the second month of the fourth year of Solomon’s reign,
\v 38 and it was completed in the eighth month of his eleventh year—meeting all his specifications and requirements. So it took \add over\add* seven years to build it.
\c 7
\s1 Solomon’s palace
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Solomon \add also\add* spent thirteen years getting his own palace finished.
\v 2 He built the ‘Lebanon Forest Hall’ that was forty-six metres long, twenty-three metres wide, and fourteen metres high, with four rows of cedar pillars and cedar beams sitting across the pillars.
\v 3 It was roofed with cedar boards on forty-five supports set out into three rows of fifteen.
\v 4 There were three rows of window gaps arranged in sets of three.
\v 5 All the entrances and doorposts were made \add with wood\add* planed with square sides, and they were arranged in sets of three.
\p
\v 6 He made his covered porch with pillars. It was twenty-three metres long and fourteen metres wide.
\p
\v 7 He made a hall for his throne where he would judge cases—\add called\add* ‘The Judgement Hall’. It was covered with cedar from floor to ceiling.
\p
\v 8 His \add actual\add* residence behind the hall was constructed in a similar way. Solomon also built a house for his wife who was Pharaoh’s daughter.
\p
\v 9 All these buildings were constructed with expensive stones that had been cut to size and trimmed with saws inside and out. They went from the foundation up to the eaves, and from the outside across to the great courtyard.
\v 10 These large, costly stones in the foundations \add were cut\add* in two sizes, with the largest almost five metres \add long\add*.
\v 11 On top of those were other costly stones of various sizes, and then cedar \add beams\add*.
\v 12 The largest courtyard had three rows of dressed stones and one row of cedar beams, as did the inner temple courtyard and the palace hall.
\s1 Huram comes to work
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 13 King Solomon sent for \add a craftsman\add* in Tyre \add called\add* Huram.\f + \fr 7:13 \ft Sometimes (including this passage) his named is spelt ‘Hiram’, but we’ve opted to consistently name him ‘Huram’ (as per 2 Chr. 4:11) to conveniently avoid confusion with King Hiram (also from Tyre).\f*
\v 14 His mother was a widow with Naphtali ancestry, and his father had been a bronze engraver in Tyre. He was very knowledgeable and skilled in everything to do with bronze, and \add so\add* he came to work for King Solomon.
\s1 The two bronze pillars
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 3:15-17)
\p
\v 15 He cast two \add hollow\add* bronze pillars—each being just over eight metres tall and measuring over five metres around.
\v 16 Then he made two decorative bronze caps for those pillars—each over two metres high.
\v 17 To hang over them, he made ornamental chains and wreaths—seven sets over the cap on each pillar.
\v 18 Then he made two rows of \add decorative bronze\add* pomegranates to go around each of the two caps.
\v 19 The two caps were each shaped like lilies that were nearly two metres high
\v 20 and there were two hundred \add bronze\add* pomegranates in rows around each cap. NEED A DIAGRAM
\p
\v 21 Huram stood the \add two\add* pillars at the temple entrance porch. (The left pillar got nicknamed ‘Yakin’ and the one on the right, ‘Boaz’.)
\v 22 The two bronze caps shaped like lilies \add were placed on\add* top of the pillars and so the pillar construction was completed.
\s1 The bronze water tank
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 4:2-5)
\p
\v 23 Huram also cast a very large round basin \add nicknamed\add* ‘The sea’ that was five metres across (so a circumference of fourteen metres) and over two metres high.
\v 24 Part of the same casting was two rows of decorative buds that went around it under the lip of the basin. There were about eighteen buds for each metre of circumference.
\v 25 Twelve bronze cattle had also been cast and the large basin was sitting on their backs. The cattle were facing outwards with three facing towards each of the four compass points.
\v 26 The basin was about 8cm thick and it had a lip around the outside like a lily blossom. It could contain around forty-four thousand litres \add of water\add*.
\s1 The bronze basin holders
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 27 Then Huram cast ten bronze carts \add for individual water basins\add*. Each one was nearly two metres long and two metres wide and just over a metre tall,
\v 28 and they had frames on the sides with panels between them.
\v 29 There were bronze lions, oxen, and winged creatures \add in relief\add* on the panels, and above and below those, wreaths were inset into the bronze.
\v 30 Each cart had two bronze axles with four bronze wheels attached to them. At each of the top four corners, there were bronze supports to hold a \add wash\add* basin, and these were also cast with \add decorative\add* wreaths.
\v 31 Inside those supports there was a round frame that protruded half a metre upwards and was inset by a quarter of a metre. It also had engravings on it inside square frames.
\v 32 The axles were part of the main casting, and the four 70cm \add bronze\add* wheels were below the panels.
\v 33 The design of the wheels was similar to chariot wheels, but with their axles, rims, spokes, and hubs all cast \add from bronze\add*.
\v 34 Each cart had four handles as part of the casting—one projecting from each corner.
\v 35 There was a 25cm \add bronze\add* band around the top of each cart as well as supports and frames—all part of the main casting.
\v 36 He engraved winged creatures, lions, and palm trees on the flat areas of the carts wherever there was space, and engraved wreaths around them.
\v 37 So that’s how he made the ten carts—all the same shape and size using the same mold.
\p
\v 38 Huram also cast ten bronze \add wash\add* basins for the ten carts—each one was almost two metres across and could hold nine hundred litres \add of water\add*.
\v 39 He put five \add basins on\add* carts on each side of the temple (north and south \add of the east-facing temple\add*), and he put the huge basin \add on its bronze cattle\add* near the south-east corner.
\v 40 Huram \add also\add* made the basins and the \add ash\add* shovels and the bowls.
\s1 The list of temple objects
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 4:11–5:1)
\p So Huram finished everything that King Solomon had requested him to make for Yahweh’s residence:
\v 41 two \add massive\add* pillars with \add large\add* decorated caps and then networks of chain over the caps,
\v 42 the four hundred pomegranates with each pillar having two strings of one hundred of them placed over them,
\v 43 the ten carts and the ten basins on them,
\v 44 the \add huge\add* water tank and the twelve cattle supporting it,
\v 45 and the pots and shovels and bowls and all the other containers.
\p All the above was made from polished bronze by Huram for King Solomon for Yahweh’s residence.
\v 46 The king \add had them\add* cast in the clay in the Jordan valley between Sukot and Tseretan.
\v 47 None of the above was weighed, because bronze was so plentiful that it didn’t matter.
\p
\v 48 Solomon also had the gold items made for Yahweh’s residence, including the gold \add incense\add* altar and the gold table for the sacred bread,
\v 49 the ten pure gold lampstands that were placed five on each side of the entrance to the inner room, along with the gold flowers, lamps, and tongs,
\v 50 the pure gold cups, trimmers, bowls, dishes, and firepans, and \add also\add* the gold door sockets for the inner holiest room and the main room of the temple.
\p
\v 51 Finally all the work that King Solomon did for Yahweh’s residence was completed, and he \add had\add* all the holy things from his father David, including the silver and the gold and the containers, taken into the storerooms of the temple.
\c 8
\s1 The sacred box is taken to the temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 5:2–6:2)
\p
\v 1 So Solomon assembled the Israelite leaders, and the tribal and clan heads, in Jerusalem \add to celebrate\add* the arrival of the box containing Yahweh’s agreement coming from David’s city, i.e., Zion.
\v 2 That all happened during the Celebration \add in Shelters\add* in the seventh month of Etanim.
\v 3 The Israelite elders and the priests lifted the \add sacred\add* box
\v 4 and carried it \add to the temple\add*. \add Other\add* priests and Levites brought all the sacred objects from the sacred tent.
\v 5 King Solomon and all the Israelites who were there, walked ahead of the box, sacrificing \add more\add* sheep and cattle than could be counted, because there were so many.
\p
\v 6 Then the priests brought the box containing the agreement into the inner room in the temple (the very holy place), and \add placed it\add* beneath the wings of the \add two wooden\add* winged creatures,
\v 7 The two creatures had their wings stretched out \add and touching in the middle of the room\add* where the \add sacred\add* box was placed, so their wings covered over the top of the box and its \add carrying\add* poles.
\v 8 The poles were \add very\add* long and the ends of them could be seen from the entrance to that inner room, but \add otherwise\add* they couldn’t be seen from the outside, and they’re still there to this day.
\v 9 There was nothing in the box except for the two stone slabs that Moses had put inside at Mt. Sinai (also called Horeb) where Yahweh made the agreement with the Israelites after he’d brought them out of Egypt.
\p
\v 10 When the priests came back out of the holy place, a cloud filled Yahweh’s residence
\v 11 and the priests were unable to stand to minister because of the cloud, because Yahweh’s presence had filled his temple.
\v 12 Then Solomon said, “Yahweh stated that he would reside in the cloudy darkness.
\v 13 Actually, I’ve built a raised house for you—where you can live forever.”
\s1 Solomon’s address to the people
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 6:3-11)
\p
\v 14 Then while all the people stood there, the king turned around and pronounced a blessing on all Israel,
\v 15 saying, “Blessed is Yahweh, the god of Israel, who spoke audibly to my father David, and then put into action the promise he’d given,
\v 16 ‘From the day when I brought my people Israel out of Egypt, I didn’t choose a city from \add among\add* all the tribes of Israel to build a house \add in\add* so that my name would be \add established\add* there. Instead I chose David to lead my people Israel.’ ”
\p
\v 17 \add Solomon\add* \add continued,\add* “My father David wanted to build a temple to confirm Yahweh’s power and authority here.
\v 18 However, Yahweh told him that it was good that he wanted to build a temple for him,
\v 19 however he wouldn’t be the one to build it, because it would be his biological son who would do it.
\p
\v 20 Now Yahweh has put his promise into action, and I’m here now having succeeded my father David—sitting on Israel’s throne just as Yahweh said, and I’ve been the one to build the residence for Israel’s god Yahweh.
\v 21 I’ve included a place for the box containing \add the two stone tablets with\add* the agreement that Yahweh made with our ancestors when he brought them out of Egypt.”
\s1 Solomon’s prayer of dedication
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 6:12-42)
\p
\v 22 Then Solomon stood between Yahweh’s altar and all the gathered Israelites, and held his arms up towards the sky
\v 23 and prayed, “Yahweh, the god of Israel, there’s no god like you in the heavens above or \add here\add* on the earth below, keeping the agreement and \add showing\add* loyal affection to your servants, the ones who serve you with honest devotion.
\v 24 You have done what you told your servant David, my father, that you said you’d do—you said it with your mouth and then put it into action with your hands as \add we can see\add* today.
\v 25 Now, Yahweh, the god of Israel, do the other things for your servant David my father, when you told him that there’ll always be one of his descendants on Israel’s throne, provided they follow you like he did.
\v 26 So now, God of Israel, show that you’re faithful to do what you say, by doing what you told your servant David, my father.
\p
\v 27 “But would God really reside on the earth? The sky and the heavens aren’t big enough for you so how could this building I’ve constructed contain you?
\v 28 But still, please give attention to your servant’s prayer and my request for favour, Yahweh my god. \add Please\add* listen to my humble prayer that your servant is praying to you today
\v 29 that your eyes be on this house night and day—the place that you’ve said will be associated with your name. May you answer the prayers that your servant will pray towards this place.
\v 30 Listen to your servant’s plea for favour, and the prayers that your people Israel will pray toward this place. Keep listening from where you live in the heavens, and hear and forgive.
\p
\v 31 “When someone \add is accused of\add* sinning against his neighbour and says on oath \add that the accusation is false\add*, and makes that oath in front of your altar here in this temple,
\v 32 then you should listen from the heavens and judge your people Israel, so that wicked people will be condemned and bear the appropriate punishment, and innocent people should be declared not guilty and dealt with appropriately.
\p
\v 33 When your people Israel are defeated by an enemy because they’ve sinned against you, and they turn back to you and confess your name and pray, and plead for favour toward you in this temple,
\v 34 then you should listen from the heavens and forgive the sin of your people Israel, and bring them back to this land that you gave to our ancestors.
\p
\v 35 “\add Or when\add* the rain is held back in the sky because your people have sinned against you, and then they pray towards this place and acknowledge you and turn away from the disobedience that caused the problem,
\v 36 then listen to them from the heavens and forgive the sin of your servants and your people Israel, because you’ll direct them towards the right way to conduct themselves. Then you’ll send rain onto your land that you gave to your people as an inheritance.
\p
\v 37 “Suppose that there was a drought in the land, or diseases like blight or mildew, or swarms of locusts or grasshoppers, or if an enemy besieges them in a gated city, or some other trouble or calamity,
\v 38 then if anyone from your people Israel who knows about their own disobedience would spread out their palms toward this temple,
\v 39 then listen to them from your heavenly abode and forgive their sin, and give them what they need. You and you alone are the only one who knows what’s inside a human heart.
\v 40 Then the people will want to obey you as long as they’re alive \add here\add* in the land that you gave to our ancestors.
\p
\v 41 “Also the foreigners who are not descendants of Israel and who came from distant countries because of your reputation
\v 42 (when they heard about your nature and saw your power in action), and they come and pray toward this temple,
\v 43 \add then\add* listen \add to them\add* from your abode in the heavens and do what that foreigner begged of you, so that all the countries will know your reputation and respect you like your people Israel do. Then they’ll know that your authority is connected with this residence that I’ve built.
\p
\v 44 “When your people go out to battle against the enemy, and on the road where you sent them they pray to \add you\add* Yahweh, in this city that you’ve chosen, with this house that I’ve built for your honour,
\v 45 Then listen from the heavens to their prayer and their request, and help their situation.
\p
\v 46 “If they disobey you (because everyone sins) and you’re angry with them and cause them to be defeated by their enemies, and then they get taken prisoner and led away to the enemy’s country (even a far away country),
\v 47 and then they have a change of mind in the land where they were taken to as captives, and while they’re still living in that land, they turn back and beg for your favour and confess, ‘We’ve disobeyed and done wrong—we’ve behaved wickedly,’
\v 48 and they return to you with all their hearts and all their souls in the land of their enemies where they were taken captive, and they pray to you toward the direction of their country which you gave to their fathers, the city that you chose, and the residence that I’ve built for your honour,
\v 49 then listen to their prayers and requests from your abode in the heavens, and help their situation.
\v 50 Forgive your people who’ve disobeyed you and forgive the wicked things that they’ve done. Help their captors to be compassionate to them,
\v 51 because \add they’re\add* your people and your special possession who you brought out from Egypt—from the middle of the iron-smelting furnace.
\p
\v 52 “May you notice the plea for favour from your servant and from your people Israel—to listen to them in all their crying out to you.
\v 53 Because you yourself separated them for yourself as a special possession out of all the people groups in the world, just as you said through your servant Moses when you brought our ancestors out from Egypt, my master Yahweh.”
\s1 Solomon blesses the people
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 54 Once Solomon was finished praying that prayer and request for favour to Yahweh, he stood up from bowing on his knees with his palms spread out toward the heavens in front of Yahweh’s altar,
\v 55 then stood \add there\add* and blessed all the Israelite people, saying,
\v 56 “Blessed be Yahweh, who has given peace to his people Israel like he said he would. He didn’t fail to do even one part out of all the good things that he told his servant Moses that he’d do.
\v 57 May our god Yahweh be with us, just as he was with our ancestors. May he not leave us or abandon us,
\v 58 help us to want to follow him and walk on all his paths and obey his commands and statutes and judgements that he gave our ancestors.
\v 59 May this prayer of mine pleading for Yahweh’s favour, be remembered by our god Yahweh day and night—to protect \add both\add* his servant and his people Israel as each day requires,
\v 60 so that all the people groups in the worlds would know that Yahweh is the god—there’s no \add other\add* besides him.
\v 61 And \add you people\add*, may your purpose be to completely follow our god Yahweh, by obeying his statutes, and by keeping his commands like you’re doing today.”
\s1 The temple dedication
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 7:4-10)
\p
\v 62 Then the king and all the Israelites offered sacrifices to Yahweh,
\v 63 and King Solomon sacrificed peace offerings to Yahweh (twenty-two thousand cattle and one hundred and twenty thousand sheep) and the king and the Israelites dedicated Yahweh’s residence.
\v 64 On that day the king reserved the middle of the courtyard that was in front of the temple, because that was where he made the burnt up offerings and the grain offerings and the fat pieces of the peace offerings. \add That was\add* because the bronze altar was too small to handle all those offerings.
\p
\v 65 Then Solomon and Israelites from all over the country celebrated in front of Yahweh at that time—\add people\add* from Levo-Hamat \add in the far north\add* down to the Egyptian border \add in the far south\add*—one week, then a second week—\add a total of\add* fourteen days.
\v 66 The next day, he instructed the people to go home, and they blessed the king, then went home feeling happy and encouraged because of all the good things that Yahweh had done for his servant David’s \add family\add* and for his people Israel.
\c 9
\s1 Yahweh speaks to Solomon again
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 7:11-22)
\p
\v 1 Once Solomon had finished the construction of the temple and his palace, and various other projects of his,
\v 2 Yahweh appeared a second time to him, similar to his appearance at Gibeon
\v 3 and said, “I’ve heard your prayer and your plea for favour requested from me. I’ve declared that this residence that you built is holy by associating my name with it forever, and my eyes and my heart will constantly be there.\f + \fr 9:3 \ft ‘my eyes and my heart’: many translations have something like ‘my presence’, but we’ve left it literal here for the reader to interpret rather than oversimplifying what we don’t necessarily understand.\f*
\v 4 And as for you, if you’ll behave with godliness just like your father David lived with total dedication and honesty, by following everything that I’ve commanded you including keeping my statutes and my judgements,
\v 5 then I’ll continue your dynasty over Israel forever, just like I told your father David when I said, ‘your descendants will never be removed from Israel’s throne.’
\v 6 If you or your descendants ever turn back from following me and don’t keep my commands and statutes, and you go and serve other gods and bow down to them,
\v 7 then I’ll cut Israel off from the land that I’ve given them and from the temple that’s dedicated to my name—I’ll send them far away, and Israel will become an example and a laughing stock among all the \add other\add* countries.
\v 8 As this temple is on a hill and very visible, everyone who passes \add anywhere\add* nearby \add will see it and\add* will be appalled and will hiss and they’ll say, ‘\add What did the people do\add* that was so bad that Yahweh did that to this country and to this temple?’
\v 9 Then they’ll answer, ‘\add It’s\add* because they abandoned their god Yahweh who brought their ancestors out of Egypt, but they held on to other gods and bowed down to them and served them. Therefore Yahweh brought all this calamity onto them.’ ”
\s1 King Hiram gains some poor cities
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 8:1-2)
\p
\v 10 It took twenty years for Solomon to construct the temple for Yahweh and the palace for himself.
\v 11 King Hiram from Tyre, had supported Solomon with cedar and cypress logs, and with gold—as much as he’d needed. So King Solomon gave him twenty cities in the Galilee region,
\v 12 but when Hiram came down from Tyre to see the cities that Solomon had given him, he wasn’t very happy about them
\v 13 and asked, “My friend, what \add sort of\add* cities are these that you’ve given me?” and he called the area ‘Kavul’ \add (meaning ‘Worthless’), which it’s still called\add* to this day.
\v 14 Hiram \add had\add* sent four tonnes of gold to Solomon.
\s1 Solomon’s other achievements
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 8:3-18)
\p
\v 15 Now this is the matter of the forced labour that King Solomon brought up to build Yahweh’s residence and his palace, the raised terraces and the Jerusalem \add city\add* wall, and \add the cities of\add* Hatsor, Megiddo and Gezer.
\v 16 (Egypt’s King Pharaoh had seized Gezer and set fire to it and killed the Canaanites who had lived in the city. Then he’d given it as a wedding present to his daughter when Solomon had married her.)
\v 17 Solomon rebuilt Gezer and lower Beyt-Horon,
\v 18 Ba’alat, Tadmor in the Judah wilderness,
\v 19 all of Solomon’s storehouse cities, and the cities for his chariots and horses. They \add also\add* built whatever else Solomon wanted—in Jerusalem, in Lebanon, and in the entire area of his dominion.
\v 20 All the remaining Amorite, Hittite, Perizi, Hivi, and Jebusite people (they weren’t descendants of Israel,
\v 21 had descendants who continued living in Israel. Those were people groups that the Israelites hadn’t been able to annihilate so Solomon used them for forced labour (\add where they remain\add* to this day).
\v 22 But he didn’t consign any Israelites to slavehood, because they were his warriors and servants, and his officers and captains, and the commanders of his chariots and horsemen.
\v 23 Some were commanders of the five hundred and fifty work supervisors.
\p
\v 24 Once Pharaoh’s daughter moved out of David’s city to the palace that he’d built for her, then he built the raised terraces.
\p
\v 25 Solomon offered burnt offerings and peace offerings three times that year on the altar that he’d built for Yahweh, and he burnt incense to Yahweh with it, and he completed the temple.
\p
\v 26 King Solomon constructed a fleet \add of ships\add* in Etsyon-Gever, which is near Eylot on the shore of the Red sea in the Edom region.
\v 27 \add King\add* Hiram sent \add some of\add* his servants who were experienced sailors to serve with Solomon’s men.
\v 28 They sailed to Ofir and brought back fourteen tonnes of gold that they took to Solomon.
\c 10
\s1 The queen from Sheba visits Solomon
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 9:1-12)
\p
\v 1 Now the queen from Sheba had heard a report about how Yahweh had blessed Solomon, and she came to test him with difficult questions.
\v 2 She came to Jerusalem with lots of her top people, camels carrying spices and gemstones and a lot of gold. Then she went to Solomon and started asking him everything that had been on her mind.
\v 3 He answered all her questions—there wasn’t any topic that he wasn’t able to shed light on.
\v 4 When the queen from Sheba saw the extent of Solomon’s wisdom, the palace that he’d built,
\v 5 the food on his table, the seating of his servants, the uniforms and the service of his waiters, and the burnt offerings that he sacrificed at Yahweh’s temple, she was totally lost for words.
\p
\v 6 She told the king, “\add Wow,\add* everything that I heard about you \add back\add* in my own country, and about how wise you are, is true.
\v 7 I hadn’t believed it until I got here and saw it with my own eyes, but then I find that what I heard is only \+em half\+em* of how incredible it is. Your wisdom and your goodness is \add way\add* more than what I’d heard described.
\v 8 Your men are so fortunate and \add especially\add* your servants who are always standing in front of you and able to hear the wise things that you say.
\v 9 May your god Yahweh be blessed, who was pleased with you \add and\add* placed you on Israel’s throne. He appointed you as king to rule them fairly and honestly because Yahweh loves Israel forever.”
\p
\v 10 Then the queen gave to king four tonnes of gold and very many spices and precious gemstones. Never again did King Solomon receive more spices than what the queen gave him \add that day\add*.
\p
\v 11 \add King\add* Hiram’s fleet also brought gold from Ofir \add as well as\add* a lot of almug wood and precious gemstones.
\v 12 The king used the almug timber to make railings\f + \fr 10:12 \ft The exact meaning of this Hebrew word is unknown, especially as it’s only used once. (Other possibilities for the use of this obviously high-quality timber includes ‘steps’ or ‘pillars’.)\f* for the temple and the palace, and lyres and harps for the musicians. Such a quantity and quality of almug wood has never again been brought in or seen \add in Israel\add* to this day.
\p
\v 13 Then King Solomon gave the queen from Sheba everything she wanted—whatever she requested on top of what he had already given to her. Then she departed with her servants and returned to her country.
\s1 Solomon’s wealth
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 9:13-29)
\p
\v 14 The amount of gold that came to Solomon in one year was \add around\add* twenty-two tonnes,
\v 15 not counting what came from explorers and traders, and all the Aravi kings, and the governors of the land.
\p
\v 16 King Solomon had two hundred body shields made from beaten gold \add overlaid over wood\add*—each shield took six kilograms of gold.
\v 17 \add He also had\add* three hundred \add smaller\add* shields made—each of them \add covered\add* with two kilograms of gold—and placed in his ‘Lebanon Forest Hall’.
\p
\v 18 The king had a large ivory throne made and overlaid with pure gold.
\v 19 It had six steps going up to it and the seat-back was rounded at the top. It had armrests on both sides, then a lion on each side next to the armrests,
\v 20 plus twelve lions standing one on each side of the six steps. There was nothing like it in any other kingdom.
\p
\v 21 All of Solomon’s cups were gold, and the various dishes in the ‘Lebanon Forest Hall’ were \add also\add* pure gold. Nothing was made out of silver because it was considered to be of little value during Solomon’s time
\v 22 because the king had a fleet of ships that joined \add King\add* Hiram’s fleet. Every three years the fleet would return bringing gold and silver, ivory, monkeys and baboons.\f + \fr 10:22 \ft baboons: We’re not certain of the meaning of this last word (some suggest ‘peacocks’), nor are we sure of the type of the ships named ‘Tarshish ships’.\f*
\p
\v 23 King Solomon became richer and wiser than any other king in the world,
\v 24 and \add even\add* people from distant countries would come to see Solomon to listen to the wisdom that God had given him.
\v 25 Every visitor would bring a gift: gold containers, clothes, weapons, spices, horses and mules. That continued year after year.
\p
\v 26 Solomon acquired 1,400 chariots and twelve thousand horsemen. He stationed \add most of them\add* in his chariot cities and \add some\add* with him \add there\add* in Jerusalem.
\v 27 \add While he was\add* king, silver in Jerusalem was given a value similar to stones, and cedar timber was given a value like that of the sycamores that grow in plenty in the lowlands.
\v 28 Solomon acquired horses from Egypt and from Kue—his traders would acquire them from Kue for the king for a price.
\v 29 An Egyptian chariot went for six hundred silver coins and a horse for one hundred and fifty. Then they’d export them again to the Hittite and Aramean kings.
\c 11
\s1 Solomon turns from God
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now King Solomon loved many foreign women—besides Pharaoh’s daughter \add there were\add* Moabite women, Ammonite women, Edomite women, Tsidoni women, and Hittite women.
\v 2 \add They were\add* from the nations that Yahweh had told Israel about, “You must not mix with people from those nations, and they must not join together with you. They will certainly entice you to worship their gods.” \add However,\add* Solomon clung to them for love.
\v 3 He had seven hundred wives who were kings’ daughters, as well as three hundred slave-wives, and his wives were instrumental in his turning away \add from Yahweh\add*.
\v 4 By the time of his old age, Solomon’s wives had persuaded him to worship the gods from their countries—his heart wasn’t totally dedicated to Yahweh like his father David had been.
\v 5 So Solomon started to worship the Sidonians’ God Ashtoret and the Ammonites’ detestable God Malkam,
\v 6 so he did many things that Yahweh had said were evil—he didn’t remain fully committed to Yahweh like his father David had been.
\v 7 Then Solomon built a place to worship Chemosh, the Moabites detestable God, on a hill facing Jerusalem, and also for Molech, the detestable god of the Ammonites.
\v 8 He did those things for his foreign wives who were burning incense and sacrificing to their gods.
\p
\v 9 Although Israel’s god Yahweh had appeared twice to Solomon, he now turned his devotion away from Yahweh, who then became angry with him.
\v 10 Yahweh had warned Solomon not to follow after other gods, but he didn’t obey Yahweh’s instructions,
\v 11 and Yahweh told him, “You have this against you: you haven’t kept my agreement and you’ve disobeyed my statutes that I gave you. \add So\add* I’ll definitely tear the kingdom away from you and give it to your servant,
\v 12 except that for the sake of your father David I won’t do it in your lifetime, rather, I’ll tear the kingdom away from your son.
\v 13 What’s more, I won’t tear the whole kingdom away—I’ll leave one tribe for your son \add to rule\add* for my servant David’s sake, and for the sake of my chosen \add city\add* Jerusalem.”
\s1 Solomon’s enemies
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh caused Hadad the Edomite (and a descendant of the Edomite king) to stand against Solomon.
\v 15 \add In the past\add* when David had been in Edom with his army commander Joab to bury their dead, they had killed every male in Edom.
\v 16 (Joab and the Israelite warriors had had to stay there for six months to kill the Edomite males.)
\v 17 However, Hadad had been a small child at that time, and he’d fled to Egypt with some of his father’s Edomite servants.
\v 18 They’d passed through Midian and then Paran where they’d gained some more men before entering Egypt where King Pharaoh gave him a house and land, and provided him with food.
\v 19 Pharaoh took a liking to Hadad and gave him his wife Queen Tahpeneis’ sister to marry.
\v 20 Tahpeneis’ sister gave birth to his son Genuvat, and Tahpeneis weaned him right there in Pharaoh’s house, so Genuvat grew up in Pharaoh’s house among his sons.
\p
\v 21 When Hadad in Egypt heard that \add both\add* David and his army commander Joab had died, Hadad said to Pharaoh, “Give me permission to leave, and I’ll go \add back\add* to my land.”
\p
\v 22 But Pharaoh asked him, “But what’s wrong with staying here? You’ve stunned me by asking to go to your land.”
\p “Nothing’s \add lacking here\add*,” he replied, “but \add I have to go\add*—please give me permission to leave.”
\p
\v 23 God \add also\add* caused Elyada’s son Rezon to stand against Solomon. He’d run away from his master, King Hadadezer in Tsovah,
\v 24 then he’d gathered men for himself and had become a commander of a raiding band. When David attacked them, they went to Damascus. They lived there and gained control of the city.
\v 25 Then during all of Solomon’s reign, he’d been outspoken against Israel—\add encouraged by\add* the evil that Hadad did. He reigned over \add Syria\add* Aram) and he loathed Israel.
\s1 God’s promise to Jeroboam
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 26 Nevat’s son Jeroboam, an Efrati from Tseredah \add also\add* rebelled against the king. (His widowed mother Tseruah was one of Solomon’s servants.)
\v 27 This is the background about how it started: Solomon had built the raised terrace and had repaired a gap in the wall of his father David’s city.
\v 28 Solomon had noticed Jeroboam as a strong and hard-working young man, so he’d appointed him as supervisor over all the forced labour from Manasseh and Ephraim’s regions.
\v 29 One time when Jeroboam was away from Jerusalem, the prophet Ahiyah the Shiloni met him on the road. He was wearing a brand-new a new cloak, and there was \add just\add* the two by themselves in the countryside.
\v 30 Ahiyah then pulled the new cloak off \add his shoulders\add* and tore it into twelve pieces,
\v 31 and told Jeroboam, “Take ten pieces for yourself, because this is what Israel’s god Yahweh says, ‘Listen, I’m about to tear the kingdom away from Solomon’s control, and I’ll give the\f + \fr 11:31 \ft Most English translations leave out this ‘the’ (which is in the Hebrew). However, we suspect that both Ahiyah and Jeroboam knew exactly which of the ten tribes it would be.\f* ten tribes to you.
\v 32 The one tribe\f + \fr 11:32 \ft It would have been obvious at the time that this was referring to Judah. (The unmentioned twelfth tribe is Benjamin—the youngest brother and the smallest tribe.)\f* will be for him, for the sake of my servant David and for the sake of Jerusalem city which I’ve chosen from all the tribes of Israel.
\v 33 \add That is\add* because they’ve abandoned me. Instead they’ve bowed down to the Sidonians’ God Ashtoret, to the Moabite god Chemosh, and to the Ammonite God Malkam. They’re \add no longer\add* following my instructions or doing what they know I’ve said is right—not \add obeying\add* my statutes or my judgements like the king’s father David did.
\v 34 But I won’t take the entire kingdom off him, but rather I’ll leave him as a leader while he’s still alive for the sake of my chosen servant David, who obeyed my commands and my statutes.
\v 35 However, I’ll take the kingship away from his son and I’ll give the ten tribes to you \add to rule\add*.
\v 36 I’ll give Solomon’s son one tribe to rule so that one of David’s descendants will always rule in my chosen city Jerusalem because it’s associated with my name.
\v 37 But I’ll use you, and you’ll be able to reign over the area where you want to rule—you’ll become king over Israel.
\v 38 Then if you’ll listen to everything that I command you, and walk on the right path and do what pleases me and keep my statutes and my commands, just like my servant David did, then I’ll be with you. I’ll establish a dynasty for you just like I did for David, and I’ll give Israel to you.
\v 39 I’ll make David’s descendants suffer because of Solomon’s behaviour, but not forever.’ ”
\p
\v 40 Solomon tried to kill Jeroboam, but he left the area and fled to Egypt. He remained in Egypt under the protection of King Shishak until Solomon’s death.
\s1 Solomon’s death
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 9:29-31)
\p
\v 41 A record of all the other things that Solomon did, and all the wise things that he said, was written in the ‘Record of Solomon’s Achievements’.
\v 42 Solomon reigned over all Israel from Jerusalem for forty years,
\v 43 then he died and was buried in David’s city, and his son Rehoboam became king.
\c 12
\s1 The north rejects Rehoboam
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (2 Chr. 10:1-19)
\p
\v 1 \add Solomon’s\add* \add son\add* Rehoboam went to Shechem, because all Israel had assembled there to make him king.
\v 2 Meanwhile, Nevat’s son Jeroboam in Egypt where he’d fled from King Solomon) heard the news and returned to Israel.
\v 3 \add The leaders of the northern tribes\add* summoned him, and they all went to petition \add King\add* Rehoboam,
\v 4 “Your father \add Solomon\add* required us to contribute too much labour. If you’ll lighten the burden on us, then we’ll serve you.”
\p
\v 5 “Come back in three days,” he replied, “and I’ll give my response.” So the people left.
\p
\v 6 Then King Rehoboam consulted with the elders (who’d served his father Solomon while he was alive), asking them for advice on how to respond.
\p
\v 7 “If you want to serve these people well,” they replied, “answer them with kindness now then they’ll serve you well in the future.”
\p
\v 8 However he decided to ignore that advice from the elders, and he consulted with the young men who were standing in front of him and that he’d grown up with,
\v 9 so he asked them, “What would you all recommend that we tell the people who wanted me to lighten the burden that my father placed on them?”
\p
\v 10 “You should tell them this,” his young companions replied. “They’ve asked that you should lighten the load on them from what your father demanded, but on the contrary, you should tell them that your little finger is thicker than your father’s thigh bone was.
\v 11 \add Tell them that\add* your father placed a heavy burden on them, but that you’ll add to that. He punished them with whips, but you’ll punish them with scorpions.”
\p
\v 12 So when Jeroboam and all the leaders came \add back\add* to \add King\add* Rehoboam on the third day as he’d instructed them,
\v 13 he answered them harshly—ignoring the advice of the elders \add at the beginning\add*.
\v 14 Instead he spoke to them according to the advice of the young men, saying, “My father placed a heavy burden on you all, but as for me, I’ll add to those demands. My father punished you all with whips, but as for me, I’ll punish you all with scorpions.”
\v 15 So the king didn’t listen to the people. That change came from Yahweh so that what he’d had Ahiyah the Shiloni tell Nevat’s son Jeroboam would \add all indeed\add* happen.
\p
\v 16 When all Israel saw that the king hadn’t listened to them, the people responded,
\q1 “What share in David do we have?
\q1 We won’t get any inheritance from Jesse’s son.
\q1 \add Go back\add* to your tents, Israel.
\q1 Look at your dynasty now, David.”
\p So the \add northern\add* Israelite leaders went home \add again\add*.
\v 17 However, Rehoboam continued to rule over the cities in Judah.
\p
\v 18 Then King Rehoboam took Adoniram who was over the forced labour, to go \add north\add* but the people killed him by throwing rocks at him and the king had to hurriedly jump into his chariot to flee to Jerusalem.
\v 19 Eber since then, the \add northern tribes of\add* Israel have rebelled against David’s dynasty.
\p
\v 20 So as soon as all Israel heard that Jeroboam had returned \add from Egypt\add*, they summoned him to their assembly and appointed him as king over all \add northern\add* Israel. Only the Judah region was left under the rule of David’s grandson \add Rehoboam\add*.
\s1 Shemayah’s prophecy
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 11:1-4)
\p
\v 21 So when Solomon’s son Rehoboam returned to Jerusalem, he assembled all the tribes of Judah and Benjamin, and 180,000 \add warriors\add* were chosen to fight against the northern tribes to return the kingship to him.
\v 22 But God told the prophet Shemayah,
\v 23 “\add Go and\add* tell Solomon’s son Rehoboam, the king of Judah, and tell all the descendants of Judah and Benjamin, and the remainder of the people, saying,
\v 24 ‘Yahweh says this: Don’t go and fight against your Israelite relatives. Everyone should just return home because everything that happened there was initiated by me.’ ” When they heard Yahweh’s message, they took note of it and everyone went back home.
\s1 Jeroboam makes gold calves
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 25 Then Jeroboam built Shechem in the Ephraim hill country and he lived there \add for a while\add*. Then he left there and built Penuel.
\v 26 After that, he worried that the entire country might want to go back under David’s dynasty.
\v 27 He worried that if the people went down to Jerusalem to offer sacrifices in Yahweh’s temple, they might shift their allegiance to King Rehoboam in Judah and then his life might be in danger.
\p
\v 28 So after getting advice, the king made two gold calves, and he told his people, “Going \add all the way\add* down to Jerusalem is too far for you. Look, \add these can be\add* your gods, Israel, who brought you out from Egypt.”
\v 29 He set one up at Bethel \add in the south)\add* and the other one at Dan \add (in the north)\add*.
\v 30 That caused the people to sin, and \add some\add* people went up as far as the one in Dan.
\v 31 He built temples on hills and appointed priests from various tribes—not just from the tribe of Levi.
\s1 The warning of worshipping there at Bethel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 32 In the eighth month on the 15th, Jeroboam put on a celebration like the one in Judah. \add He’d made\add* an altar at Bethel and he got the priests from the hill temples he’d made to come and stand there, then he offered a sacrifice on that altar to the calves that he’d made.
\v 33 That date of the 15th day of the eighth month was one that he’d just decided for himself, and he instituted a \add new\add* celebration for the Israelites and he burnt incense on the altar \add as a sacrifice\add*.
\c 13
\p
\v 1 Then a prophet from of God came from Judah, \add taking\add* Yahweh’s message to Bethel. as Jeroboam was standing by the altar to burn incense.
\v 2 The prophet called out against the altar with Yahweh’s message: “Altar! Altar! Yahweh says this: Listen, \add one of\add* David’s descendants will have a son who’ll be named Josiah, and he’ll sacrifice the priests from the hill temples on you—the ones who are burning incense on you. \add Yes,\add* human bones will be burnt on you.”
\v 3 Then he offered them evidence that what he said came from Yahweh, “Listen, that altar will be broken into pieces, and the ashes in it will spill all over the ground.”
\p
\v 4 The moment the King Jeroboam heard the message that the man of God had called out against the altar at Bethel, he called for the man to be arrested. However, the arm that he used to point to him over the altar suddenly became paralysed and he wasn’t able to pull it back.
\v 5 Then the altar \add itself\add* broke open and ashes poured out \add onto the ground\add*, exactly as Yahweh had promised through that man of God.
\v 6 Then the king begged the prophet, “Please, pray to your god Yahweh for me, so my hand will return to normal.”
\p So the man of God interceded with Yahweh, and the king was able to use his hand again.
\v 7 “Come home with me,” the king told the prophet, “and have something to eat. Then I’ve got a gift to give you.”
\p
\v 8 But the man of God replied, “\add Even\add* if you offered me half of your house, I wouldn’t go with you, and I wouldn’t eat or drink in this place.
\v 9 Yahweh already told me not to eat or drink \add here\add*, and not to return home by the same road I came on.”
\v 10 Then he \add left for home\add* taking a different route from the one he’d used to come to Bethel.
\s1 The old prophet from Bethel
\s4 Both kingdoms
\p
\v 11 Now \add at that time there was\add* an old prophet who lived in Bethel, and his sons\f + \fr 13:11 \ft This is singular in the Hebrew, but the rest of this segment suggests multiple sons.\f* had been and told him all the news about what that man of God had done that day in Bethel and they told him the message that had been passed on to the king.
\v 12 “\add Oh,\add* what road did he take when he left?” their father asked, and \add indeed\add* his sons had noticed which road the man of God from Judah had taken.
\v 13 So he told his sons to saddle his donkey, and he rode \add off\add*
\v 14 after the man of God, and found him sitting under an oak tree. “Are you the man of God,” he asked him, “who came from Judah?”
\p “I am,” he said.
\p
\v 15 “Come home with me and have something to eat.” he invited him.
\p
\v 16 “\add Sorry,\add*” he replied. “I can’t go with you or visit your home, and I can’t eat or drink with you in this place,
\v 17 because Yahweh instructed me not to eat or drink here, or to go home by the same road that I came on.”
\p
\v 18 Then the old prophet told him, “I’m also a prophet like you, and Yahweh sent a messenger to tell me that I should take you home with me so you can have something to eat and drink.” (But that was a lie.)
\p
\v 19 So the prophet accompanied him to his house and had a meal there.
\v 20 Then while they were still seated at the table, Yahweh spoke through the old prophet,
\v 21 and he called out to the man of God who’d come from Judah, “Yahweh says this: Because you have been rebellious against Yahweh and because you didn’t obey what Yahweh instructed you
\v 22 and you’ve returned and you’ve had food and drink in the place where he told you not to eat or drink, your body won’t be buried in your home town.”
\p
\v 23 After the meal was finished, the old prophet saddled the donkey for the prophet that he’d brought back,
\v 24 and he left. But on the road, a lion came across him and killed him. His corpse ended up lying on the road with the donkey standing beside it, and the lion also standing beside it.
\v 25 Some passers-by saw the corpse on the road and \add the unusual sight of\add* the lion standing beside it, and when they got to the city where the old prophet lived, they told everyone the news.
\p
\v 26 When the prophet who had brought him back from the road heard he said, “That will be the man of God who was rebellious against Yahweh. Yahweh must have given him to the lion, and it tore him into pieces and put him to death according what Yahweh had said would happen.”
\v 27 Then he told his sons, “Saddle the donkey for me.” They put the saddle on,
\v 28 and he went and found the body lying on the road, and the donkey and the lion standing by it. The lion hadn’t eaten any of the man of God’s body, nor had it attacked the donkey.
\v 29 The prophet lifted the body and laid it across the donkey and brought it back to his city to be buried in his own tomb.
\v 30 They wailed over him, saying, “Oh dear, my brother,” and they laid his body in the old prophet’s grave.
\v 31 When the period of mourning was over, he told his sons, “When I die, bury me in the grave where the man of God is buried. My bones will end up beside his bones.
\v 32 The message from Yahweh that he shouted out against the altar in Bethel will certainly happen, and against all the hilltop worship places in Samaria.
\s1 Jeroboam’s fatal sin
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 33 Despite the man of God’s warning, \add King\add* Jeroboam didn’t stop his evil ways—\add in fact\add* he chose more people from across the country to become priests. Anyone who wanted to be a priest would be appointed and he would become one of the priests of the hilltop worship places.
\v 34 Those were the sins of Jeroboam that would eventually lead to the rejection and destruction of his entire family.
\c 14
\s1 The death of Jeroboam’s son
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 At that time, \add one of\add* Jeroboam’s sons, Abijah, became \add very\add* sick
\v 2 and Jeroboam told his wife, “Go and get changed so that no one will recognise you as my wife. Then go to Shiloh where the prophet Ahiyah is—he’s the one who prophesied that I’d become king.
\v 3 Take ten loaves of bread and \add some\add* cakes and a jar of honey to give to him. He’ll tell you what will happen to the boy.”
\p
\v 4 So his wife got ready and went to Ahiyah’s house at Shiloh. Ahiyah was now very elderly and not able to see,
\v 5 but Yahweh had told him, “Listen, Jeroboam’s wife will be coming to ask for your guidance about her son, because he’s sick. I want you to tell her so-and-so. And when she comes, she won’t be dressed like a queen.”
\p
\v 6 So as soon as Ahiyah heard her walk through the doorway, he said, “Come in, wife of Jeroboam. Why did you get changed like that? Now I’ve been instructed to pass bad news onto you—
\v 7 go \add back\add* and give Jeroboam this message from Israel’s god Yahweh, ‘I chose you out from the people and placed you as a leader over my people Israel,
\v 8 and I tore the kingdom away from David’s descendants and gave it to you. But you haven’t been like my servant David who obeyed my instructions and followed me with integrity—only doing what he knew that I approved of.
\v 9 However, you’ve done more evil \add things\add* than any of those who ruled before you. You’ve rejected me and made cast images of other gods, and have provoked me into anger.
\v 10 Because of that, I’m about to bring calamity to your family: I’ll cause all your male descendants to die, either before or after they’ve left home. I’ll eliminate your family just like \add dried\add* dung burns until it’s all gone.
\v 11 The \add family members\add* who die in the city will be eaten by dogs, and those who die in the countryside will be eaten by birds. \add All this\add* comes from Yahweh.’
\p
\v 12 So go back home, \add but\add* as soon as you set foot in the city, your son will die.
\v 13 All Israel will mourn for him and they’ll bury him. He’ll be the only one of your children to be buried, because out of all of Jeroboam’s descendants, he’s the only one that Israel’s god Yahweh found good in.
\v 14 In the future, Yahweh will appoint for himself, a king over Israel who will get rid of Jeroboam’s descendants—\add in fact\add* it’s beginning to come about even now.
\v 15 And Yahweh will strike Israel just like the reeds bend over in the swamps, and he’ll uproot Israel from this good soil that he gave to their ancestors and he’ll scatter them beyond the Euphrates, because they made their Asherah \add statues\add* which provoked Yahweh to anger.
\v 16 He will hand Israel over \add to their enemies\add* because of Jeroboam’s sins and \add how\add* he encouraged Israel to sin.”
\p
\v 17 So Jeroboam’s wife left and went home to Tirtsah. As she entered the doorway of their home, their son died.
\v 18 Just as Yahweh had told them, all Israel mourned for the boy and he was buried.
\s1 Jeroboam’s death
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 19 Everything else that Jeroboam did, including the record of wars that his army fought and how he ruled, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 20 Jeroboam reigned for twenty-two years, then he died and was buried with his ancestors, and his son Nadav replaced him as king.
\s1 Rehoboam rules in Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 11:5–12:15)
\p
\v 21 Meanwhile, Solomon’s son Rehoboam reigned in Judah. He was forty-one when he \add started\add* reigning, and he reigned for seventeen years from Jerusalem—the city that Yahweh had chosen to associate his name with out of all the tribes of Israel. Rehoboam’s mother was Na’amah the Ammonite.
\p
\v 22 \add The people of\add* Judah did what Yahweh had decided was evil—\add in fact\add* they made him more jealous \add (by worshipping other gods)\add* than their ancestors ever had.
\v 23 They even built hilltop temples and sacred pillars and poles on every high hill and under every large evergreen tree.
\v 24 There were also male prostitutes associated with them, and the Israelites did the same abhorrent things as the nations that Yahweh had removed ahead of \add their entering the land\add*.
\p
\v 25 In the fifth year of King Rehoboam’s \add reign\add*, Egypt’s King Shishak attacked Jerusalem
\v 26 and took away the valuables from Yahweh’s temple, and the king’s palace. He took everything \add of value\add*, including all the gold shields that Solomon had made.
\v 27 To replace them, King Rehoboam made \add some\add* bronze shields and assigned them to the officers who protect the entrance to the palace.
\v 28 Whenever the king would enter the temple, the guards would quickly take the shields there, and when he left, they’d run them back to the palace.
\p
\v 29 Everything else that Rehoboam did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 30 Rehoboam and Jeroboam were in a continual state of war \add during their reigns\add*.
\v 31 Then Rehoboam (his mother was Na’amah the Ammonite) died and was buried with his ancestors in David’s city, and his son Aviyam replaced him as king.
\c 15
\s1 Aviyam rules over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 13:1–14:1)
\p
\v 1 In the eighteenth year of (Nevat’s son) King Jeroboam’s \add reign over Israel\add*, Aviyam became king of Judah.
\v 2 (His mother’s name was Ma’akah, \add one of\add* Absalom’s daughters.) He reigned from Jerusalem for three years
\v 3 but he disobeyed God in ways similar to his father—he wasn’t interested in following his god Yahweh completely like his ancestor David had done.
\v 4 However for David’s sake, his god Yahweh gave Aviyam a son \add to rule\add* after him—to allow Jerusalem to continue on with a flicker of light
\v 5 because David had done what Yahweh had said was correct, and throughout his life he hadn’t deviated from what Yahweh had instructed him, except in the matter concerning Uriah the Hittite.
\v 6 Rehoboam’s and Jeroboam’s factions were in a continual state of war \add during Aviyam’s reign\add*.
\v 7 Everything else that Aviyam did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah, as the war between Aviyam and Jeroboam continued.
\p
\v 8 Then Aviyam died and was buried with his ancestors in David’s city, and his son Asa replaced him as king.
\s1 Asa’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 14:1-5; 15:16–16:6, 11-14)
\p
\v 9 In the twentieth year of King Jeroboam’s \add reign over\add* Israel, Asa became king of Judah
\v 10 and reigned from Jerusalem for forty-one years. (His grandmother was Absalom’s daughter Ma’akah.)
\v 11 Asa did what was pleasing to Yahweh, like his ancestor David \add had done\add*,
\v 12 and he expelled the male prostitutes from the country and removed all the idols made by the previous generations.
\v 13 He also removed his grandmother Ma’akah from being queen-mother because she had made a horrid Asherah goddess, then he cut down her horrid idol and burnt it in the Kidron valley.
\v 14 He didn’t remove the hilltop temples, \add but despite that\add* he was fully devoted to Yahweh all his life.
\v 15 He brought everything that father had dedicated, plus more of his own, into Yahweh’s temple, \add including\add* gold, silver, and containers.
\p
\v 16 Asa and King Baesha of Israel were in a continual state of war during their reigns.
\v 17 King Baesha invaded Judah and fortified Ramah so that no one could pass in or out to help King Asa of Judah.
\v 18 So Asa took all the remaining gold and silver from the temple and palace storerooms, and sent it with his servants to take to Ben-Hadad, son of Tavrimon, son of King Hezyon of Syria who lived in Damascus, to tell him,
\v 19 “You and me have an agreement between us that was made by our two fathers, so look, I’ve sent a present of gold and silver to you. So then, break your agreement with King Baesha of Israel so he’ll give up on attacking me.”
\p
\v 20 Ben-Hadad did what king Asa requested, and he sent the commanders of his best warriors to attack Israelite cities including Iyon, Dan, Abel-Beyt-Ma’akah, and all Kineret along with all Naphtali region.
\v 21 When \add King\add* Baesha heard about that, he stopped fortifying Ramah and withdrew to Tirtsah.
\p
\v 22 Then King Asa informed all Judah that they were needed at Ramah. They removed the stones and timber from Baesha’s fortifications, and king Asa used the materials to build Geva (in Benjamin) and Mitspah.
\v 23 Everything else that Asa did, and all his great works and all the cities he built, are described in the book of the events of the kings of Judah. However in his old age, he developed problems with his feet.
\v 24 Then he died and was buried with his ancestors in his ancestor David’s city and his son Jehoshaphat replaced him as king.
\s1 Nadav’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 25 In the second year of Asa’s reign over Judah, Jeroboam’s son Nadav became king of Israel where he reigned for two years.
\v 26 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, following his father’s behaviour and his sin that \add in turn\add*, caused Israel to sin.
\p
\v 27 \add A man named\add* Baesha (from the Issachar tribe) plotted against King Nadav and killed him at the Philistine city of Gibton when Nadav and all Israel were besieging the city.
\v 28 It was the third year of \add King\add* Asa’s reign in Judah when Baesha killed King Nadav, then Baesha took over as king \add of Israel\add*.
\v 29 As soon as he took over, he had Jeroboam’s entire family executed—not leaving a single one of them alive. That fulfilled what Yahweh had said through his servant Ahiyah the Shiloni,
\v 30 because Jeroboam’s \add own\add* sins, as well as his causing Israel to sin, had provoked Yahweh into getting angry.
\p
\v 31 Everything else that Nadav did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 32 \add King\add* Asa and King Baesha of Israel were in a continual state of war during their reigns.
\s1 Baesha’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 33 Ahiyah’s son Baesha \add began\add* to reign over Israel in the third year of King Asa’s \add reign\add* over Judah, and he ruled from Tirtsah for twenty-four years.
\v 34 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, similar to Nevat’s son Jeroboam’s behaviour and his sin that \add in turn\add*, caused Israel to sin.
\c 16
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Hanani’s son Jehu to tell Baesha,
\v 2 “I took a nobody and promoted you to leader over my people Israel, yet you followed Jeroboam’s behaviour and provoked me to anger by causing my people Israel to sin,
\v 3 so now I’ll get rid of you and your family—I’ll do the same to you that I did to Jeroboam and his family.
\v 4 \add The bodies of\add* those in your family who die in the city will be eaten by dogs, and those who die in the countryside will be eaten by vultures.
\p
\v 5 Everything else that Baesha did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 6 When Baesha died he was buried in Tirtsah, and his son Elah replaced him as king.
\p
\v 7 Yahweh sent that message via Hanani the prophet’s son Jehu. It was for Baesha and his family—speaking against the evil that they’d done that had provoked Yahweh to get angry with their behaviour which was as bad as Jeroboam’s family had been. Yahweh was also angry with Baesha because he had killed all of Jeroboam’s family.
\s1 Elah’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 8 Baesha’s son Elah \add began\add* to reign in the twenty-sixth year of King Asa’s \add reign\add* over Judah, and he ruled from Tirtsah for two years.
\v 9 However, his servant Zimri who was the commander of half of the chariots conspired against him. Once when Elah was in Tirtsah getting himself drunk at the house of Artsah (who was in charge of the palace),
\v 10 Zimri went in and attacked and killed him, and took over his place as king. (That was in the twenty-seventh year of King Asa’s \add reign\add* over Judah.)
\p
\v 11 As soon as Zimri took over and sat on his throne, he had all of Baesha’s family executed—he didn’t leave a single male or relative or friend.
\v 12 \add In that way,\add* Zimri eliminated all of Baesha’s descendants just as Yahweh had said via the prophet Jehu
\v 13 concerning all of Baesha’s sins and the sins of his son Elah, and because they caused Israel to sin by provoking Israel’s god Yahweh to get angry with their idols.
\v 14 Everything else that Elah did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\s1 Zimri’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 15 Zimri reigned from Tirtsah for seven days in the twenty-seventh year of King Asa’s \add reign\add* over Judah. \add At the time,\add* the Israelite army were besieging the Philistine city of Gibton,
\v 16 and when the army camped \add around the city\add* heard that Zimri had rebelled and assassinated the king, that day all the people in the camp installed the army commander Omri \add as king\add* to reign over Israel.
\v 17 So Omri took all his warriors from Gibton and besieged Tirtsah.
\v 18 As soon as Zimri realised that the city was surrounded, he went into the saferoom in the palace and set fire to the building and died in the flames
\v 19 because he’d done what Yahweh had said was evil—following Jeroboam’s behaviour including causing Israel to sin.
\v 20 Everything else that Zimri did, including his rebellion, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\s1 Omri’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 21 At that time, the people of Israel were divided concerning their leadership—half of them supported Ginat’s son Tivni as king, and the other half supported Omri,
\v 22 but those supporting Omri prevailed, so Tivni was executed and Omri \add remained\add* in power.
\v 23 Omri became king in the thirty-first years of King Asa’s \add reign\add* over Judah, and reigned over Israel for twelve years—six of them from Tirtsah.
\v 24 He bought a hill from \add a man named\add* Shemer for seventy kilograms of silver, then built \add his capital\add* city on it. He named it Samaria in honour of Shemer who’d owned the hill.
\p
\v 25 Omri did what Yahweh had said was evil—\add in fact\add* he did more evil things than all those \add kings\add* who preceded him.
\v 26 He followed (Nevat’s son) Jeroboam’s behaviour including causing Israel to sin by provoking Israel’s god Yahweh to get angry with their idols.
\v 27 Everything else that Omri did, including his military victories, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 28 Then Omri died and was buried in Samaria, and his son Ahab replaced him as king.
\s1 Ahab’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 29 Omri’s son Ahab became king of Israel in the thirty-eighth year of King Ahab’s \add reign\add* over Judah, and he reigned Israel from Samaria for twenty-two years.
\v 30 Ahab did what Yahweh had said was evil—\add in fact\add* he did more evil things than all those \add kings\add* who preceded him.
\v 31 As if following Jeroboam’s behaviour wasn’t bad enough, he married Jezebel, the daughter of the Tsidoni King Etba’al, then went and served Baal and bowed down to him.
\v 32 He built a temple for Baal in Samaria and placed an altar in it.
\v 33 Ahab \add also\add* made an Asherah pole and went on to provoke Israel’s god Yahweh to get angry, more than any of the previous kings of Israel \add had done\add*.
\v 34 During his reign, Hiel from Bethel rebuilt Jericho city. As they laid the foundations, his eldest son Aviram died, and when they hung the city gates, his youngest son Seguv died, just as Yahweh had told (Nun’s son) Joshua.
\c 17
\s1 Elijah prophesies about a coming drought
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add One day the prophet\add* Elijah from the Tishbim in the Gilead \add region\add* said to Ahab, “As Israel’s god Yahweh lives, the one who I serve, there won’t be any rain or \add even\add* dew these \add next\add* years unless I give the command.”
\v 2 Then Yahweh told Elijah,
\v 3 “Get away from here and travel eastward towards the Kerit stream that enters the Jordan \add river\add* to hide.
\v 4 You’ll be able to drink from the stream, and I’ve commanded the ravens to bring food to you there.”
\p
\v 5 So he did what Yahweh told him, and went and lived by the Kerit stream near the Jordan.
\v 6 Ravens brought bread and meat to him \add every\add* morning and again in the evening, and he was able to drink from the stream.
\v 7 However, the stream dried up after a while because there was no rain anywhere in the country.
\s1 Elijah and the widow at Zarephath
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh told Elijah,
\v 9 “Leave here \add and\add* go to Zarephath in the Sidon \add region\add* and stay there. Listen, I’ve commanded a widow lady there to keep you fed.”
\v 10 So he got ready and went to Zarephath. As he entered the town, he saw a woman picking up bits of firewood, and he asked her, “Can you please get me a drink of water.”
\v 11 As she went to get it, he called out, “Would you have a piece of bread to go with it?.”
\p
\v 12 But she replied, “As your god Yahweh lives, I don’t have any bread at all—only a handful of flour in the jar, and a little bit of oil in the jug. See, I’m gathering a few bits of firewood so I can make a little bread for me and my son. After we’ve eaten that, then we’ll die.”
\p
\v 13 But Elijah told her, “Don’t be worried. Go and do what you said, except make some bread for me first and bring it out to me. Then after that, you can make some for you and for your son,
\v 14 because Israel’s god Yahweh has told me that your flour won’t run out and your oil won’t come to an end until the day when Yahweh gives us rain again.”
\p
\v 15 So the woman went and did what Elijah said, then \add sure enough\add* Elijah and the widow and her household had enough food every day.
\v 16 The jar of flour never got empty and the jug of oil never ran out, just as Yahweh had promised via Elijah.
\p
\v 17 Some time afterwards, the woman’s son got sick and then it got so bad that he died,
\v 18 so she hassled Elijah, “You man of God, why did you come here! Now my sins have been brought to God’s mind, and he’s caused my son to die!”
\p
\v 19 But he told her to bring her son to him, and taking the boy from her arms, he took the body upstairs to the room he was staying in, and laid him down on his bed.
\v 20 Then he called out to Yahweh, “Yahweh, my god, would you really bring tragedy to this widow that I’m staying with by causing her son to die?”
\v 21 Then three times he stretched himself over the boy’s body and begged Yahweh, “Yahweh, my god, please let this child’s life return into his organs.”
\v 22 Yahweh took notice of Elijah and the boy’s life returned and he revived.
\p
\v 23 Then Elijah carried the boy back downstairs and gave him to his mother and told her, “Look, your son’s alive.”
\p
\v 24 “Now I \add certainly\add* know that you’re a man of God,” the woman responded, “And when you say that you’re speaking for Yahweh, it’s really true.”
\c 18
\s1 Elijah versus Baal’s prophets
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the third year of drought, Yahweh told Elijah to go and show himself to King Ahab and tell him that it would soon rain,
\v 2 so he went to present himself to Ahab.
\p \add Meanwhile\add* in Samaria, the food shortage was terrible
\v 3 and \add King\add* Ahab had summoned his palace manager Obadiah. (Obadiah was a sincere follower of Yahweh.
\v 4 When \add Queen\add* Jezebel had tried to kill Yahweh’s prophets, Obadiah had hidden a hundred prophets—fifty in each of two caves and kept them supplied with food and water.)
\v 5 \add King\add* Ahab told Obadiah, “\add We’ll\add* go across the country to all the springs and all the valleys that water sometimes flows in. Perhaps we’ll find \add some\add* grass so we can keep a horse or a mule \add or two\add* alive and not lose our breeding stock.”
\v 6 So they divided the country \add into two\add*, and they each went off in a different direction.
\p
\v 7 While Obadiah was on a path, Elijah \add came to\add* meet him. Obadiah recognised him and fell to his knees with his face to the ground, and asked, “Is it \add really\add* you, my master Elijah?”
\p
\v 8 “\add Yes,\add* it’s me,” he replied. “\add Now\add* go \add and\add* tell your master that I’m here.”
\p
\v 9 “How have I sinned,” Obadiah responded, “that you’re giving your servant a death sentence?
\v 10 As your god Yahweh lives, my master sent people to every kingdom to try to find you, and if they said you weren’t there, then he made them promise under oath that it was true.
\v 11 So now you tell me to tell my master that you’re here,
\v 12 but as soon as I leave you, Yahweh’s spirit will take you to goodness knows where. Meanwhile I’ll go and tell Ahab, and he’ll come here and not find you, so then he’ll kill me, even though I’ve been trying to obey Yahweh since I was young.
\v 13 Haven’t you heard, my master, what I did when Jezebel was killing Yahweh’s prophets and I hid a hundred of them in two caves and kept them supplied with food and water?
\v 14 Yet now you’re trying to get me killed by telling my master that he’ll find you here.”
\p
\v 15 “As Commander-in-chief Yahweh lives—the one I serve,” Elijah replied, “I’ll definitely be here to see him today.”
\p
\v 16 So Obadiah went \add and\add* informed Ahab who then came to meet Elijah.
\v 17 When he saw Elijah, Ahab asked him, “Is that you—the one who causes trouble for Israel?”
\p
\v 18 “It’s not me who brings trouble onto Israel,” Elijah replied. “But rather it’s you and your father’s families who decided to ignore Yahweh’s commands and serve the Baals instead.
\v 19 So now, get all Israel to go to Mt. Carmel, along with the 450 prophets of Baal and the 400 prophets of Asherah who \add Queen\add* Jezebel feeds at her table.”
\p
\v 20 So Ahab summoned all the Israelites and the prophets to go to Mt. Carmel,
\v 21 and Elijah addressed the people, “How much longer are you all going to remain unsure who to follow? If Yahweh is God, follow him, but if it’s Baal, follow him.” But the people just stayed silent.
\v 22 Then he said, “I’m Yahweh’s only remaining prophet, but Baal has 450 prophets.
\v 23 Let them get us two bulls and they can choose one for themselves. Then they can cut it into pieces and lay them a pile of wood that they’ve set for a fire, but they can’t light the fire. I’ll do the same with the other bull, but also won’t light the fire.
\v 24 Then they must call to their god and I’ll call to Yahweh, and the god who answers by setting the wood on fire must be the true God.”
\p “It sounds good to us,” the people answered.
\p
\v 25 So Elijah told Baal’s prophets, “\add Okay,\add* there’s a lot more of you guys, so you choose your bull and go first and call out to your god, but don’t set fire to the pile.”
\p
\v 26 So they took the bull he gave them and prepared it all, and then they called out to Baal from morning until midday, shouting, “Baal, answer us!” But they didn’t hear any voice and got no answer. Then they hopped around the altar they’d made.
\p
\v 27 Finally at noon, Elijah \add began\add* mocking them, saying, “Call out louder because he’s a god. Maybe he’s thinking about something else, or busy with something, or travelling somewhere. Perhaps he’s asleep and needs waking up.”
\v 28 So they shouted even louder and did their usual cutting of their bodies with knives and swords until a lot of blood flowed.
\v 29 Midday passed and they continued raving all afternoon, but they didn’t hear any voice, and they got no answer or even any suggestion that their god was listening.
\p
\v 30 Then Elijah told the people, “\add Okay, now\add* come over here by me.” So all the people came over by him, and he repaired the altar of Yahweh that had been torn down.
\v 31 He took twelve stones (the number of the sons of Jacob who Yahweh told would be called Israel)
\v 32 and used them to rebuild Yahweh’s altar. Then \add around\add* the altar he made a trench large enough to hold the water to water in two drums of seed.\f + \fr 18:32 \ft A more Jewish interpretation that seems to agree with v34–35—see https://hermeneutics.stackexchange.com/questions/31555/water-in-elijahs-trench.\f*
\v 33 He arranged the firewood, and cut the bull into pieces and placed them on top,
\v 34 and then ordered, “Fill four jars with water and pour it on the burnt offering and the firewood.” Then he said “\add Now\add* do it a second time,” and they did it a second time, and \add again\add* he said, “Do it a third time,” and they did it a third time.
\v 35 The water ran around the altar and even filled the trench.
\p
\v 36 When it was time to offer the evening sacrifices, the prophet Elijah approached \add the altar\add* and prayed, “Yahweh, God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, let it be known today that you are God in Israel and I’m your servant, and I’ve done all these things following your instructions.
\v 37 Answer me, Yahweh! Answer me, so that this country will understand that you, Yahweh, are God, and that you can turn their hearts back again.”
\p
\v 38 Then Yahweh sent fire down from the sky that consumed the pieces of meat, the firewood, the stones, and the dirt \add around the altar\add*, including \add all\add* the water that had been in the trench.
\v 39 When all the people saw what had happened, they fell onto their knees and bowed their faces to the ground, saying, “Yahweh is God! Yahweh is God!”
\p
\v 40 Then Elijah ordered them, “Grab hold of Baal’s prophets. Don’t let any of them sneak away.” So they seized them, and Elijah brought them down to the Kishon valley and he slaughtered them there.
\s1 The drought ends
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 41 Then Elijah told \add King\add* Ahab, “Get ready and have something to eat and drink, because I can hear the noise of heavy rain \add coming\add*.”
\v 42 So Ahab got ready and had something to eat and drink, but Elijah went further up \add Mt.\add* Carmel and \add squatted\add* with his face down between his knees
\v 43 and told his servant, “Go up now, \add and\add* look towards the ocean.”
\p He went up and looked and said, “There’s nothing there.”
\p That happened seven times,
\v 44 but on the seventh time he came back and said, “Wow, there’s a small cloud out above the ocean about the size of a man’s palm.”
\p Then Elijah ordered him, “Rush \add and\add* tell Ahab to get his chariot ready and head down so he won’t be caught in the rain.”
\p
\v 45 Very soon the sky was full of dark clouds, then wind and heavy rain. Ahab \add kept\add* riding and made it to Jezreel \add city\add*.
\v 46 Yahweh gave Elijah extra strength and he tucked his robe into his belt, and ran ahead of Ahab going into Jezreel.
\c 19
\s1 Elijah flees to Mt. Sinai
\p
\v 1 Then \add King\add* Ahab told Jezebel everything that Elijah had done, including how he’d killed all of Baal’s prophets,
\v 2 so she sent this message to Elijah, “May the gods kill me or do worse if I haven’t done with your life what you did to those prophets by this time tomorrow.”
\v 3 Hearing that, he packed up and fled for his life to Beersheba in Judah , where he left his servant.
\v 4 Then he went an extra day’s travel further \add south\add* into the wilderness where he sat down under a broom bush and prayed that he might be allowed to die, saying, “This is now too much, Yahweh. Take my life, because I’m no better than my ancestors.”
\p
\v 5 Then he laid down and slept under the broom bush, until he was suddenly woken by a messenger \add from Yahweh\add* telling him to get up and eat.
\v 6 Elijah looked \add around\add* and wow, there was some bread near his head that had been cooked on glowing coals, and a jug of water. He got up and ate and drank, and then lay down again.
\v 7 Then Yahweh’s messenger returned a second time and prodded him and said, “Get up \add and\add* eat, because \add otherwise\add* the journey \add ahead\add* will be too much for you.”
\v 8 So he got up and ate and drank. That food lasted his for forty days as he travelled to God’s mountain, Horeb.
\v 9 He entered a cave there and overnighted in the cave, then suddenly Yahweh spoke to him, asking, “What are you doing here, Elijah?”
\p
\v 10 “I’ve been very zealous for \add you\add*, Commander-in-chief Yahweh,” he replied. “But the Israelite people have abandoned your agreement, and they’ve torn down your altars and killed your prophets with the sword. So \add here\add* I am, left by myself while meanwhile they’re trying to kill me.”
\p
\v 11 Then Yahweh said, “Go out and stand on the mountain in front of me, Yahweh. Then look, I’m about to go past you.” Then there was an incredibly strong wind that hit the mountain and broke off bits of rock that then shattered. \add But\add* Yahweh wasn’t in the wind, and after the wind there was an earthquake \add but\add* Yahweh wasn’t in the earthquake \add either\add*.
\v 12 After the earthquake there was a fire, \add but\add* Yahweh wasn’t in the fire.
\p Then after the fire, there was the sound of a soft whisper.
\v 13 When Elijah heard that, he wrapped his cloak around his face and went out and stood at the opening of the cave, and he heard a voice ask him, “Why are you here, Elijah?”
\p
\v 14 “I’ve been very zealous for \add you,\add* Commander-in-chief Yahweh,” he replied, “But the Israelite people have abandoned your agreement, and they’ve torn down your altars and killed your prophets with the sword. So \add here\add* I am, left by myself while meanwhile they’re trying to kill me.”
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh told him, “Go back towards the Damascus wilderness, then you must go and anoint Hazael as king over Syria.
\v 16 Also you need to anoint Nimshi’s son Jehu as king over Israel, and you must anoint Shafat’s son Elisha from Abel-Meholah to succeed you as prophet.
\v 17 In the future, anyone who escapes from being killed by Hazael’s \add army\add* will be killed by Jehu’s \add army\add*, and anyone who escapes from being killed by Jehu’s \add army\add* will be killed by Elisha.
\v 18 But I’ve \add still\add* got seven thousand people in Israel who’ve never bowed to Baal or kissed his image.”
\s1 Elijah inspires Elisha
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 19 So Elijah left that place and went and found Shafat’s son Elisha. There were twelve teams ploughing a field and he was with the back pair \add of cattle\add*. Elijah \add walked\add* past him and threw his cloak over him.
\v 20 Elisha left the plough and ran after Elijah, saying, “Please let me kiss my father and my mother, then I’ll come with you.”
\p “Go on back then,” Elijah replied, “because I haven’t done anything for you.”
\p
\v 21 So Elisha went back and killed his pair of cattle. He used the wood of the plough to cook the meat on, and he shared it around the people. After they’d eaten, he packed up and followed Elijah and became his helper.
\c 20
\s1 Ben-Hadad attacks Samaria
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add At that time,\add* King Ben-Hadad of Syria joined with thirty-two smaller kingdoms and they brought their combined armies and horses and chariots and besieged Israel’s capital Samaria \add ready\add* to attack it.
\v 2 He sent messengers to Israel’s King Ahab \add who was inside\add* the city
\v 3 to tell him, “Ben-Hadad says that your gold and silver are mine, as well as the best of your wives and sons.”
\p
\v 4 “Whatever you say, my master the king,” replied Israel’s king. “I’m yours along with everything I have.”
\p
\v 5 Then the messengers returned, saying, “Ben-Hadad said that you’ll give him your gold and silver and your wives and sons,
\v 6 but around this time tomorrow, he’ll send his servants to you to search through your palace and the homes of your people, and they’ll take everything that they consider to be of value.”
\p
\v 7 So Israel’s king summoned all Israel’s leaders and told them, “Listen and note how this man is looking for trouble. He’s claiming my wives and my sons, and my gold and silver, and I didn’t contradict him.”
\p
\v 8 But the elders and all the others responded, “\add No,\add* don’t listen \add to him\add* and don’t consent \add to his demands\add*.”
\p
\v 9 So Ahab told Ben-Hadad’s messengers, “Tell my master the king that everything he demanded the first time, I’ll do, but I can’t agree to this new demand.”
\p So they returned \add to Ben-Hadad\add* with that response
\v 10 and Ben-Hadad sent another message, “The gods can do whatever they like to me and more if I leave enough dust in Samaria for all my prisoners to have a handful each.”\f + \fr 20:10 \ft Most translations interpret ‘all the people at my feet’ to be Ben-Hadad’s (large) army (which appears to go back at least to the Vulgate ‘sequitur’), but we’ve TENTATIVELY interpreted it as his (future) prisoners/slaves.\f*
\p
\v 11 Israel’s king replied, “Tell \add Ben-Hadad\add* that someone who’s heading to battle shouldn’t boast like someone relaxing after his victory.”
\p
\v 12 Ben-Hadad received that message while he was in the huts drinking with the \add other\add* kings, and he \add immediately\add* ordered his men to get into position so they got ready to attack the city.
\p
\v 13 Just then, a prophet approached Israel’s King Ahab and told him, “Yahweh asks if you’ve seen that great army, because he’s going to give you victory over them today so you’ll know that Yahweh is God.”
\p
\v 14 “Who will enable the victory?” Ahab asked.
\p “Yahweh says \add it’ll be\add* the young men under the provincial commanders,” he answered.
\p “Who should lead the attack?” he asked.
\p “You \add should\add*,” the prophet replied.
\p
\v 15 So Ahab gathered the provincial commanders’ young men and there were 232 of them. He \add also\add* assembled the fighting men and there were \add merely\add* seven thousand of them.
\p
\v 16 They exited the city at midday, while Ben-Hadad and the \add other\add* kings were \add still\add* getting drunk in their huts.
\v 17 The young men under the provincial commanders led the way out, and \add the scouts\add* that Ben-Hadad had sent out informed him that men were coming out of Samaria.
\v 18 He ordered, “Whether they’ve come out for peace or for battle, capture them alive.”
\p
\v 19 The provincial commanders’ young men exited the city followed by the warriors
\v 20 and each of them killed their opponent. Then the Arameans took off with the Israelites chasing them, but King Ben-Hadad escaped on a horse along with other riders.
\v 21 Then Israel’s king \add led more men out\add* and they attacked the horses and chariots—thoroughly defeating Aram.
\p
\v 22 Afterwards \add that same\add* prophet went \add back\add* to King Ahab and told him, “Go \add back and\add* fortify your defenses and plan your strategy, because Syria’s king will attack you again at the same time next year.”
\s1 Ben-Hadad’s second attack
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 23 Meanwhile the servants of Syria’s king told him, “Their gods are the gods of the hills, so that’s why they were stronger than us. But let’s fight them on the plain instead then we’ll be sure to defeat them.
\v 24 So this is what you should do: relieve those kings of their command and replace them with military commanders.
\v 25 Then gather an army like the one that was defeated, with as many horses and chariots as you had. Then we’ll fight them on the plain and we’ll be sure to defeat them.”
\p Ben-Hadad took their advice and put the plan into action.
\v 26 The following year, he assembled his army and went to Afek to battle the Israelites,
\v 27 and the Israelite warriors assembled and received their supplies. Then they went to confront them, but they seemed like ants in comparison to the massive Aramean army.
\p
\v 28 A man of God approached Israel’s king and told him, “Yahweh says that the Arameans have been saying that he’s a god of the hills, but not of the valleys. Because of that, he’s going to help you defeat this huge army, and then you’ll know that Yahweh is God.”
\p
\v 29 The two armies remained camped opposite each other for seven days, then the battle began and the Israelites killed a hundred thousand Aramean soldiers that day.
\v 30 The remainder fled into Afek, but the \add city\add* wall fell onto the remaining twenty-seven thousand men.
\p Ben-Hadad ran into the city and hid in an inner room,
\v 31 but his servants told him, “Listen, we’ve heard that the Israelite kings show mercy. Please let us wrap \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth around our waists and go out to Israel’s king with ropes on our heads—perhaps he’ll spare your life.”
\v 32 So they wrapped their waists in sacking and put ropes on their heads, and went to Israel’s king, saying, “Your servant Ben-Hadad asks you to spare his life.”
\p “Is he still alive?” he asked. “He’s like a brother to me.”
\p
\v 33 The men were looking for an indication \add of mercy\add* and when they heard that they were encouraged. “\add Yes\add*, Ben-Hadad is \add like\add* your brother,” they said.
\p “Yes, bring him \add here\add*,” he responded.
\p So Ben-Hadad revealed himself and was brought into Ahab’s chariot,
\v 34 and he told Ahab, “I’ll return the cities that my father took from with yours, and your traders can set up stalls in Damascus, just as my father did in Samaria.”
\p “Okay, I’ll accept that agreement and release you,” \add Ahab said,\add* and after formalising the agreement, he sent him away.
\s1 A prophet condemns Ahab
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 35 \add One day,\add* a prophet in training with a group told his companion, “By Yahweh’s command, please wound me.” But the man refused to slash him,
\v 36 so the \add first\add* prophet told him, “Because you didn’t obey Yahweh’s command, listen, as soon as you leave here you’ll be attacked by a lion.” Then after he left, a lion found him and killed him.
\p
\v 37 Then he found another prophet and said, “Strike me, please,” and the man struck him \add with a sword\add*—wounding him.
\v 38 Then the \add wounded\add* prophet disguised himself with a covering over his eyes and went and stood on the road \add to wait\add* for the king.
\v 39 When the king came past, the prophet called out to him, saying, “Your servant went out in the middle of the battle, and suddenly, a man came across with a captive and told me, ‘Guard this man. If he goes missing, your life will be \add forfeit\add* in place of his, or else you’ll be fined three thousand pieces of silver.’
\v 40 But while I was busy with other things, the man escaped.”
\p “\add Well,\add* by your own testimony, you deserve your punishment,” King Ahab responded.
\p
\v 41 Then he quickly uncovered his eyes and the king recognised that he was one of the prophets,
\v 42 and the prophet told him, “Yahweh says that because you released a man who should have been executed, then your life \add will be required\add* in place of his, and your people in place of his people.”
\p
\v 43 Then the king went \add on\add* to his house in Samaria, \add but he was\add* angry and depressed inside.
\c 21
\s1 Navot’s vineyard
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Sometime after that, King Ahab from Samaria \add was in his\add* palace in Jezreel \add city\add*, and his neighbour was the Yizre’eli Navot with his vineyard.
\v 2 Ahab pressured Navot, “Give me your vineyard so I can make it into my vegetable garden because it’s right beside my house. I can replace it with a better vineyard, or if you prefer, I can pay you in silver for this one.”
\p
\v 3 But Navot replied, “Far be it for me to disobey Yahweh by giving up the inheritance entrusted to my ancestors.”
\p
\v 4 So Ahab went \add back\add* to his palace bitter and angry because of Navot’s refusal to turn over the land. He refused to eat and went to bed with his face towards the wall,
\v 5 but his wife Jezebel went in and demanded, “What’s this about eating nothing and going to bed in a sulk?”
\p
\v 6 “Well, I talked with Navot the Yizre’eli,” he replied. “I offered to pay cash for his vineyard or to exchange it for another one if he preferred, but he wouldn’t let me have it.”
\p
\v 7 “\add What!\add* You’re the king of Israel!” his wife responded. “Get up \add and\add* have something to eat and cheer up! I’ll make sure myself that you get Navot’s vineyard.”
\p
\v 8 So she wrote letters in Ahab’s name and sealed them with his seal. Then she sent them to the city elders and leading citizens who lived near Navot.
\v 9 This is what she wrote, “Announce a fast, and seat Navot hear the head of the table.
\v 10 Then seat two troublemakers opposite him, and get them to testify against him that he cursed God and the king. Then take him outside and pelt him with rocks until he’s dead.
\p
\v 11 Then the elders and leading citizens of the city did what Jezebel had instructed them via the letters:
\v 12 They announced a fast and seated Navot near the head of the table,
\v 13 then two worthless men were seated opposite him, and they testified in front of \add all\add* the people that Navot had cursed God and the king. So Navot was led out of the city and killed by having rocks thrown at him,
\v 14 and they sent \add a message\add* to Jezebel to tell her that Navot had been stoned to death.
\p
\v 15 As soon as Jezebel heard that Navot had been executed, she told Ahab, “Go on now \add and\add* take over Navot’s vineyard that he refused to sell you, because he’s not around any more—he’s dead.”
\v 16 As soon as Ahab heard that Navot was dead, he went to the vineyard to take possession of it.
\p
\v 17 Then Yahweh told Elijah from Tishbeh),
\v 18 “Leave here \add and\add* go to Israel’s king Ahab in Samaria. Listen, he’s there in Navot’s vineyard, where he’s gone to take possession of it.
\v 19 Tell him that Yahweh’s asking if he’s taking possession \add of the property\add* of a murdered man. Then tell him that Yahweh wants him to know that the dogs will lick his blood in the same place where Navot died and the dogs came and licked up his blood.”
\p
\v 20 \add When Elijah arrived\add*, Ahab asked him, “\add Ah,\add* my enemy. Have you found me?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* I’ve found you,” Elijah replied. “It seems you’ve sold your soul by doing what Yahweh’s finds to be evil.
\v 21 \add Yahweh says that\add* he’s about to send disaster on you to destroy you and every male in your household—whether they’re slaves or free.
\v 22 Your \add entire\add* family will be eliminated like the families of Nevat’s son Jeroboam and Ahiyah’s son Baesha because you’ve provoked \add Yahweh’s\add* anger and made Israel sin.
\v 23 Also concerning Jezebel, Yahweh says that the dogs will eat her body by Jezreel’s \add city\add* wall.
\v 24 \add The bodies of\add* any of your family who die in the city will be eaten by dogs, and any who die in the countryside will be eaten by birds.”
\p
\v 25 Surely there was never anyone else as evil as Ahab who sold his soul by doing what Yahweh was said was wrong—albeit often incited by his wife Jezebel.
\v 26 He \add also\add* behaved disgustingly by worshipping idols like the Amorites had done \add before\add* Yahweh removed them from the land as the Israelites entered.
\p
\v 27 As soon as Ahab heard that message, he tore his clothes and dressed in sacking and fasted. He \add even\add* slept in the \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth, and he walked around slowly \add with his head down\add*.
\p
\v 28 Then Yahweh asked Elijah from Tishbeh),
\v 29 “Have you noticed that Ahab has shown repentance towards me? Because he’s humbled himself, I won’t bring that disaster on his family while he’s still alive, \add but rather\add* I’ll do it during his son’s reign.”
\c 22
\s1 prophet Mikayah and King Ahab
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (2 Chr. 18:2-27)
\p
\v 1 Then \add almost\add* three years went by without war between Syria and Israel,
\v 2 but in the third year, Judah’s King Jehoshaphat went up north to visit Israel’s king.
\p
\v 3 \add While they were talking,\add* King Ahab said to his servants, “Do you know that Ramot-Gilead should be ours, but we haven’t reclaimed it from Aram’s king?”
\v 4 Then he asked Jehoshaphat, “Will you go to war with us to reclaim Ramot-Gilead?”
\p “You and me are together,” Jehoshaphat told Israel’s king. “My people are your people, and my horses are your horses.”
\v 5 Then he added, “But let’s ask Yahweh \add about it first\add*.”
\p
\v 6 So Israel’s king gathered about four hundred prophets together and asked them, “Should I attack Ramot-Gilead, or not?”
\p “Go ahead, because our master\f + \fr 22:6 \ft Note that they don’t mention ‘Yahweh’ here.\f* will give you victory,” they answered.
\p
\v 7 But Jehoshaphat asked, “Is there no prophet of Yahweh still \add around\add* here that we can ask?”
\p
\v 8 “There’s still one man who speaks for Yahweh,” Ahab replied to Jehoshaphat, “Yimla’s son Mikayah, but I myself hate him because he never prophesies anything good about me—only negative things.”
\p “The king shouldn’t talk like that,” said Jehoshaphat.
\p
\v 9 So Israel’s king ordered an official to fetch Yimla’s son Mikayah straight away.
\p
\v 10 Now Israel’s king \add Ahab\add* and Judah’s king Jehoshaphat were both wearing their royal robes and sitting on thrones at a gate in Samaria’s city wall, and many prophets were speaking messages to them.
\v 11 Kena’anah’s son Zedekiah made some horns for himself out of iron and said, “Yahweh says that you’ll gore Syria with these until they’re destroyed.”
\v 12 All the \add other\add* prophets were prophesying similarly, saying \add things like\add*, “\add Yes,\add* attack Ramot-Gilead and be successful, and Yahweh will restore it back to you.”
\p
\v 13 Meanwhile the messenger who had gone to call Mikayah told him, “Listen, please, all the other prophets agree together as they prophesy victory for the king. Please let your message be similar to theirs and be positive.”
\p
\v 14 “As surely as Yahweh lives,” Mikayah responded, “I’ll only tell Ahab what Yahweh tells me to say.”
\p
\v 15 When he got to King \add Ahab\add*, the king asked him, “Mikayah, should we attack Ramot-Gilead, or not?”
\p “Go ahead and be successful,” he replied, “and Yahweh will give it back to the king.”
\p
\v 16 But \add looking at him,\add* the king said to him, “How many times do I have to make you promise that you’ll only tell the truth about me when you pass on Yahweh’s messages?
\p
\v 17 “\add Well,\add*” he said, “I saw all Israel scattered into the hills like sheep without a shepherd, and Yahweh said that they have no leaders so they should all return peacefully to their homes.”
\p
\v 18 “\add Ah,\add*” King Ahab told \add King\add* Jehoshaphat, “Didn’t I tell you that he wouldn’t prophesy \add anything\add* good about me—only bad news?”
\p
\v 19 But Mikayah continued, “Listen to Yahweh’s message then: I saw Yahweh sitting on his throne, and all the heavenly army were standing at both sides of him.
\v 20 Then Yahweh asked who could deceive Ahab to attack Ramot-Gilead so that he’d get killed there. Some suggested one thing and others suggested something else.
\v 21 Then a spirit came out and stood in front of Yahweh and offered to deceive \add the king\add*, and Yahweh asked him how he intended to do it.
\v 22 He replied that he’d go and be a false spirit so the kings prophets would tell lies, and Yahweh said that that would work and told him to go ahead and do it.
\v 23 So now, listen, Yahweh has put a lying a spirit into all these prophets of yours, and Yahweh has declared disaster on you.”
\p
\v 24 Then Zedekiah Kena’anah’s son) went over and slapped Mikayah on the face, and said, “Since when did Yahweh’s spirit leave me and start speaking through you?”
\p
\v 25 “Listen \add here\add*,” Mikayah replied, “you’ll find that out soon enough when you sneak into an inner room to try and hide.”
\p
\v 26 King Ahab commanded, “Take Mikayah to Amon, the city commander, and to my son Yoash.
\v 27 Tell them that the king orders them to imprison this man and limit his bread and water until I return safely.”
\p
\v 28 “If you do return safely,” Mikayah remarked, “then Yahweh hasn’t spoken through me.” And he added, “Take note all you people.”
\s1 Ahab’s death
\s4 Northern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 18:28-34)
\p
\v 29 So Israel’s King \add Ahab\add* and Judah’s King Jehoshaphat left to go and attack Ramot-Gilead,
\v 30 and Ahab told Jehoshaphat, “I’ll go into the battle incognito, but you can wear your royal robes.” So Israel’s king changed into ordinary clothes before heading into the battle.
\p
\v 31 Meanwhile, Syria’s king had ordered his thirty-two chariot commanders, “Don’t fight with the rank and file—only attack Israel’s king.”
\v 32 As soon as the chariot commanders saw Judah’s King Jehoshaphat, they thought to themselves, “Surely that’s Israel’s king.” So then they came over to attack him, but Jehoshaphat yelled out,
\v 33 and as soon as they realised that he wasn’t the king of Israel, they stopped chasing him.
\v 34 But one man casually shot an arrow with his and it struck Israel’s king between the plates of his body armour, and he called to his chariot driver, “Turn around and get me out of the battle because I’ve been wounded.”
\p
\v 35 The battle carried on all that day and King \add Ahab\add* was propped up in his chariot opposite Aram, however he died that evening and the blood from his wound pooled into the bottom of his the chariot.
\v 36 Then the call went through the battle lines just as the sun was going down, “\add Every\add* man \add should abandon the battlefield and\add* return to his home”
\p
\v 37 So King \add Ahab\add* died and was taken back to Samaria where he was buried.
\v 38 They washed out the chariot beside the pool in Samaria (where the prostitutes bathed themselves), and dogs licked up the blood, just as Yahweh had said would happen.
\p
\v 39 Everything else about Ahab and everything that he did, including the ivory palace and all the cities that he had built, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 40 Now that Ahab was dead, his son Ahazyah replaced him as king.
\s1 Jehoshaphat’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 20:31–21:1)
\p
\v 41 Asa’s son Jehoshaphat \add started\add* his reign over Judah when King Ahab \add had ruled\add* Israel for four years.
\v 42 Jehoshaphat was thirty-five when he became king and he reigned from Jerusalem for twenty-five years. (His mother was Shilhi’s daughter Azuvah.)
\v 43 He followed his father Asa in every way in doing what Yahweh wanted—he didn’t veer away from it, except that they didn’t demolish the hilltop temples so the people were still sacrificing and burning incense at \add those\add* places.
\v 44 Jehoshaphat did make a peace treaty with Israel’s king.
\p
\v 45 Everything else about Jehoshaphat, including his great achievements and his battle record, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah,
\v 46 and \add including how\add* he banished the male prostitutes from the land who had remained from the time of his father Asa.
\p
\v 47 \add At that time,\add* there was no king in Edom—a governor \add appointed by Jehoshaphat\add* ruled there.
\p
\v 48 Jehoshaphat had trading ships made to go to Ofir for gold, but they didn’t go because the ships got wrecked at Etsyon-Gever.
\v 49 Ahab’s son Ahazyah had said to Jehoshaphat, “Let my servants go with your servants in the ships.” But Jehoshaphat hadn’t accepted the offer.
\p
\v 50 Then Jehoshaphat died and was buried with his ancestors in his ancestor David’s city, and his son Jehoram replaced him as king.
\s1 Ahazyah’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 51 Ahab’s son Ahazyah \add began to\add* reign over Israel from Samaria in the seventeenth year of King Jehoshaphat’s \add reign over\add* Judah, and he reigned over Israel for two years.
\v 52 He followed the behaviour of his father and mother and Nevat’s son Jeroboam, and did what Yahweh had said was evil—causing Israel to sin like they had.
\v 53 He served Baal and bowed down to him, and he provoked Israel’s god Yahweh to get angry, similarly to everything that his father had done.
